window.LING406_TRANSCRIPTS_V2 = {"revision":"ling406-v2-slide-aligned","scripts":{"speech1-01":["**Speech perception** turns changing sound into a linguistic message. The sound reaching the ear is continuous, but the words recognized through it contain distinctions such as /b/ versus /p/.","Categorical perception concerns a mismatch between continuous physical change and discontinuous judgments. A smoothly changing sound does not have to produce a smoothly changing word label.","The central question is how the listener extracts stable categories from variable input. Formants, synthetic speech, and labeling and discrimination experiments make that question testable.","The physical side can be described through resonant frequencies and transitions. The psychological side is measured by what listeners identify and which differences they can discriminate.","Keeping those descriptions separate makes the experiment understandable: construct the sound first, measure the response, and then ask how the two are related."],"speech1-02":["A speech waveform contains overlapping movements of the vocal tract rather than neatly separated letters. The spectrogram spreads this signal across time and frequency, making some of its structure visible.","The same message can be retrieved from very different physical stimuli. A child, an adult, and another talker do not have to produce the same frequencies for the listener to recognize the intended words.","Different speakers can express the same message with very different voices. Vocal-tract anatomy, speaking rate, and surrounding sounds change the physical input without necessarily changing the words.","The mess includes variation that is informative and variation that is incidental to the word. A spectrogram makes energy visible, but it does not insert the word boundaries or phoneme labels that recognition must recover.","Recognition therefore requires more than matching the sound to one exact recording. The listener must preserve differences that distinguish linguistic categories while tolerating other variation.","A useful representation must therefore generalize across instances. It must also remain sensitive to a difference that changes the word, rather than treating all variation as irrelevant."],"speech1-03":["An **analog signal** varies continuously: a frequency or a timing interval can change by small amounts. A **symbolic representation** groups that variation into units the language system can use, such as a phoneme.","Turning a highly variable, analog signal into something more abstract means changing the level of description. An acoustic measurement specifies a physical property; a phoneme category specifies how the sound functions in a language.","**Categorical perception** is one proposed bridge between these descriptions. Several physically different sounds may receive the same label, while a small change near a boundary produces a different label.","Translation from physical to symbolic does not mean that the ear literally hears written letters. It means that varying tokens can activate the same category representation used in recognizing words.","The proposed benefit is a stable input to later language processes. Whether the system completely discards fine acoustic detail is a separate question from whether its final labels look categorical.","There are two claims to separate: the final response may be categorical, and the processing that creates that response may discard detail. Later experiments test whether the second claim follows from the first."],"speech1-04":["**Formants** are resonant frequency regions amplified by the vocal tract. Changing the shape of the tract changes which frequencies are emphasized in the outgoing sound.","Amplification of particular resonant frequencies creates the formant pattern. The sound source supplies energy, while the vocal tract favors some frequency regions over others.","The vocal-tract drawings pair different articulatory shapes with different resonance patterns. The relationship is lawful: the shape acts as a filter rather than adding an arbitrary linguistic label.","Changing tongue or mouth position changes the shape of that filter. The illustrated profiles therefore connect articulatory differences with patterns in the output spectrum.","A **formant transition** is a movement in one of these resonances over time. A steady formant helps characterize a vowel; a transition can reveal the articulatory movement into that vowel.","A transition adds time to the description. Instead of asking only where a resonance is, it asks where that resonance starts and how it moves as one articulatory configuration changes into another."],"speech1-05":["A **spectrogram** places time horizontally and frequency vertically. Dark bands show concentrations of acoustic energy; the formants are prominent bands that differ across the listed vowels.","The words keep a similar consonantal frame while the vowel changes. This makes the contrast easier to follow than comparing unrelated words with many simultaneous differences.","Compare the vowels in bead, bid, bade, bed, and bad, then the remaining examples. Their identity changes with the pattern of resonances, rather than with the presence of a single universal vowel frequency.","Horizontal position tracks the passage of time within each recording. Vertical position tracks frequency, and a darker region indicates stronger energy at that moment and frequency.","F1 and F2 provide a useful description of these contrasts. Their combination matters: hearing one band in isolation would throw away part of the vowel pattern.","Compare the relative positions of the bands, rather than counting each band as another sound. Several resonances can be present simultaneously within one vowel."],"speech1-06":["The /u/ recording has a low first formant, around 300 Hz, and a low second formant, around 900 Hz. These are two resonances of the same vowel, not two different spoken sounds.","The cursor is on the first formant, so the frequency it identifies belongs to a resonance rather than directly to the speaker's pitch. The next resonance is the second formant.","The waveform shows pressure variation over time; the spectrogram below it shows where energy is concentrated in frequency. The cursor identifies F1 within that display.","Both low resonances occur within the /u/ vowel. Their relationship contributes to its quality; they are not two alternative responses the listener must choose between.","This example gives a concrete starting point for synthesis: keep the source constant, change selected resonance frequencies, and hear how the vowel changes.","The synthesis demonstration starts from this actual recording so the later changes have an interpretable baseline. A manipulated filter can then be compared with the filter that reproduced the original vowel."],"speech1-07":["The automatic tracks estimate the changing formant frequencies through the recording. Five tracks are identified. The first three are usually important for phonemic processing, although the demonstration concentrates on the first two.","Praat can automatically find formants by estimating the resonance tracks through time. The drawn tracks summarize how those resonances change while the speaker continues producing the vowel.","F1 is the lowest formant, F2 is the next, and higher numbers refer to higher resonances. These labels specify their order, not separate speakers or successive syllables.","Only the first three are usually important for phonemic processing in this discussion, and the demonstration isolates the first two. That choice keeps the manipulation small enough to connect with the resulting sound.","Tracking the resonances makes it possible to describe a vowel with a small set of changing acoustic properties and then manipulate those properties deliberately.","Formant numbers order the resonances from lower to higher frequency. They do not tell how many syllables the speaker said or which moment came first."],"speech1-08":["The **source** is the sound that the vocal tract filters. Removing the estimated filter from the recording leaves a more buzz-like signal with less of the original vowel quality.","Extracting the source uses a model of the filter based on the formant frequencies. The procedure estimates what the recording would be like without the original resonance shaping.","The extraction uses the formant pattern to estimate what the tract contributed. The result is an approximate source that can be sent through different filters.","The more-or-less unfiltered version sounds like a buzz because much of the vowel-specific spectral pattern came from the filter. The source can still supply the excitation needed to build another sound.","This separates two causes of the final sound: the excitation that supplies energy and the resonances that shape its spectrum. Changing the second while holding the first fixed tests the contribution of the filter.","This decomposition is useful because one component can be reused. The experimenter does not need a different person to produce each vowel and thereby introduce uncontrolled changes in several properties at once."],"speech1-09":["The first filter approximates the resonances of the original recording. Another raises F1, and another raises F2 more substantially. Each manipulation changes which frequencies receive amplification.","The first filter preserves the original recording's first four formants. The other illustrated filters selectively move the first or second formant while leaving the same underlying source available.","Because the source is held fixed, a resulting vowel difference can be traced to the filter change. Raising F1 and raising F2 are distinct manipulations, even though both alter vowel quality.","Raising the first formant and raising the second formant are different interventions. Each changes a particular part of the resonance configuration rather than merely making the whole recording louder.","The displays represent filters through time. They are instructions for shaping the source, rather than three naturally recorded speakers whose voices differ in many uncontrolled ways.","The causal comparison is source held constant, filter changed. If the heard vowel changes, the altered resonance pattern is the manipulated explanation for that difference."],"speech1-10":["Applying each filter to the same source produces a new sound. The resulting spectrograms show how the resonance pattern changes when a selected formant is moved.","Multiplying the source by the filters gives new vowels. The filtered outputs show the energy pattern that results when the same excitation is shaped in different ways.","The important comparison is between a constant source and different filters. A changed vowel quality shows that vocal-tract resonance patterns are sufficient to alter what vowel the listener hears.","The new vowel does not require a completely new acoustic source. The filter changes which portions of the source are emphasized, producing a recognizable difference in vowel quality.","This is a controlled demonstration of **source–filter synthesis**: manipulate a defined acoustic cause, then compare both the physical output and its perceptual identity.","Connect the three stages in order: begin with the original recording, estimate its source, and apply controlled resonance patterns. The output is then available for both acoustic inspection and listening."],"speech1-11":["A **vowel space** represents vowels using formant values, commonly F1 and F2. Each vowel occupies a region containing many productions, rather than one exact pair of frequencies.","Formant values are partly determined by anatomical features, rates of speech, and context. The phones before and after a vowel can change the tract's movement into and out of the vowel.","Anatomy, rate, and neighboring phones move a production within this space. Regions can overlap, so a measured formant value does not always uniquely identify a vowel.","A cloud represents a distribution of productions. Its center describes a typical region, while its width shows why a category cannot be reduced to one frequency pair.","Steady formants can still generate recognizable approximations. The listener's task is to recognize useful structure in variable distributions rather than demand physically identical examples.","For a specific talker speaking carefully, F1 and F2 can still give a useful vowel-space description. Useful structure and imperfect separation can both be present in the same acoustic representation."],"speech1-12":["The **pattern playback** device converts a visible acoustic pattern into a speech-like sound. A drawn pattern specifies how energy should be distributed over frequency and time.","The pattern playback synthesizer allowed a researcher to begin with a visible sound pattern and turn it into an audible stimulus. The researcher could specify a pattern that natural speech production would be difficult to vary in perfectly equal steps.","Its role is to let researchers control the stimulus instead of relying only on naturally spoken syllables. A chosen transition can be altered while other portions of the pattern remain constant.","That control matters for a perception experiment. Differences in a listener's responses can be compared with differences the experimenter deliberately programmed into the signal.","Synthetic speech makes a causal question possible: when just one acoustic property changes, does perceived phoneme identity change smoothly or in categories?","The device supports a continuum instead of only a few endpoint recordings. Intermediate sounds are essential for asking where categories change and how discrimination behaves around those changes."],"speech1-13":["The stop consonants /b/, /d/, and /g/ involve different places of closure. Their audible release into a vowel contains rapidly changing formant information as the articulators move.","Stop consonants are distinguished by formant transitions in the demonstration. The audible transition reflects the tract opening out of a closure toward the following vowel.","Liberman and colleagues used synthetic patterns to reproduce these transitions. The listener hears a syllable even though the researcher can specify the transition independently.","The consonant's place of articulation affects that movement, so /b/, /d/, and /g/ can leave different transition patterns even when followed by a similar vowel.","The critical cue is therefore not only a steady vowel resonance. The direction and starting position of a transition can help distinguish consonants leading into a similar vowel.","Reproducing those patterns synthetically connects production with perception. The experiment changes a proposed acoustic cue and measures which consonant listeners report."],"speech1-14":["F1 stays constant while the starting frequency of F2 changes in equal steps. This creates a **continuum**: adjacent stimuli differ physically by the same amount.","F1 is kept constant while F2 onset frequency varies linearly. Linearly means that the programmed step from one stimulus to its neighbor is the same size across the continuum.","Toward one region listeners hear /b/, in another /d/, and toward the other end /g/. The equal physical spacing lets perception be compared across different regions fairly.","The manipulation therefore does not build a physical discontinuity at the place where a listener changes labels. A category boundary must be identified from the responses.","If a pair across a category boundary is easier to distinguish than an equally separated pair within a category, its advantage cannot be explained simply by a larger programmed acoustic difference.","A fixed-separation pair can be moved across the continuum. Comparing those pairs asks whether category position changes sensitivity after the size of the acoustic difference has been controlled."],"speech1-15":["A simple noncategorical observer is increasingly uncertain as a stimulus moves away from a category's best example. Its labels change gradually with distance through the continuum.","Category prototypes are particularly clear examples of the alternatives. Moving away from one prototype increases ambiguity under this simple noncategorical prediction.","The middle /d/ region rises and falls because there are categories on either side. Such a peaked labeling curve can arise merely because the task requires choosing among a small set of responses.","The requirement to choose among /b/, /d/, and /g/ can create distinct response regions even if the underlying percept changes continuously. A label curve is therefore partly shaped by what the task asks the participant to report.","Labeling by itself therefore does not settle how finely the sounds are perceived. A second task must ask whether equal acoustic differences remain equally noticeable throughout the continuum.","The prototype-distance prediction concerns gradual uncertainty, not an acoustic boundary programmed into the sound. Its implications become clearer when matched-distance discrimination is measured separately."],"speech1-16":["In a **discrimination** task, listeners report whether two sounds are the same or different. Pairs can be selected with a constant step separation throughout the continuum.","Labeling asks which category a token belongs to; discrimination asks whether two tokens differ. Giving different instructions can reveal information that one response format hides.","If perception preserves the same sensitivity everywhere, discrimination should stay roughly constant across equally separated pairs. Labeling may vary, but crossing a label boundary should not create a special perceptual advantage.","If perception of differences in F2 onset were constant over the whole continuum, equally separated pairs would have comparable discrimination accuracy. Their position relative to a label boundary would add no special advantage.","This prediction links the two tasks: categorical perception instead predicts better discrimination where the labels switch, with worse discrimination inside a category.","The contrasting prediction is a peak near a boundary. That would mean two matched acoustic changes can be treated differently depending on the categories their members receive."],"speech1-17":["The labeling functions change sharply between /b/, /d/, and /g/. The discrimination functions peak near those changes rather than staying flat across the acoustic continuum.","The obtained discrimination curves follow the locations where labeling switches. This links the ability to hear a difference with the assignment of phoneme categories.","A within-category pair can sound similar even when its physical separation equals that of a boundary-crossing pair. Increasing the step separation raises discriminability overall, but the boundary pattern remains important.","Within-category pairs receive the same label and tend to be harder to distinguish. Across-category pairs are more likely to receive different labels and show better discrimination at a matched physical distance.","This combination defines the classic evidence for **categorical perception**: abrupt category assignments together with discrimination tied to those assignments. The relationship between the graphs is the key result.","Read the labeling and discrimination panels together. A sudden label switch alone describes categorization; the corresponding discrimination peaks supply the classic categorical-perception relationship."],"speech1-18":["The demonstration separates two judgments. First, identify a sound along a continuum, such as beach versus peach; then judge whether two selected sounds differ.","The participant hears an individual continuum token for identification and a selected pair for discrimination. Those are separate trials with different requested judgments.","A label boundary is located where identification changes. A discrimination comparison asks whether pairs near that location are easier to distinguish than equally separated pairs farther from it.","In identification, the response helps locate a boundary between the alternatives. In discrimination, a pair's distance and its placement relative to that boundary are the variables of interest.","The two responses answer different questions. A listener can repeatedly choose one word while still showing some sensitivity to acoustic differences among tokens given that word label.","The demonstration makes categorical perception an observable comparison. The relevant question is not simply whether beach and peach can be recognized, but how the intermediate stimuli are grouped and distinguished."],"speech1-19":["The identification panels show how word choices vary across the continuum. The paired discrimination panels show responses to equally separated stimulus pairs.","Each identification panel locates a transition from one word response to another. Each discrimination panel organizes pairs by the part of the continuum they sample.","Find the steep portion of each person's identification curve, then compare it with the location of that person's discrimination peak. The relevant match is between a boundary and improved discrimination.","Compare within a listener before drawing a group conclusion. Two people's most sensitive pairs may occur at different physical positions because their boundaries are not perfectly aligned.","Individual curves do not have to align at exactly the same stimulus number. Averaging listeners with different boundaries can soften a pattern that is sharper within a person.","The pattern of interest is the correspondence, not a required stimulus number. A boundary-related discrimination peak can occur even when the boundary differs from another person's."],"speech1-20":["Different listeners can place the boundary at different points. Despite that variation, discrimination is generally strongest for sounds assigned to different categories.","Only different trials were included to make the short exercise workable. The correct physical judgment was different on every discrimination trial.","In the abbreviated class task, discrimination included only physically different pairs. Reporting different was therefore correct on every trial, even when a listener could not hear the difference.","This does not mean the correct judgment was easy. A participant can hear two physically different tokens as effectively the same when their difference falls within a perceptual category.","This detail explains why the graph is a measure of detected difference under this particular design. It also distinguishes the class exercise from a full task containing both same and different trials.","Individual boundary variation also changes which pair counts as between-category. The class pattern should be interpreted using each listener's identification curve rather than assuming one universal dividing point."],"speech1-21":["Bear–pear and beach–peach do not always produce identical curves. A listener may use the stimuli differently, and short experiments produce variable estimates from relatively few trials.","The research question is how the procedure might be improved, not whether every noisy line should be treated as a new perceptual law. Short tasks can sample some comparisons unevenly.","Mixing same and different trials would make response tendencies easier to assess. Repeating enough pairs and examining each person's labeling boundary would also clarify the relationship between the tasks.","Adding genuine same trials would help separate sensitivity from a tendency to answer different. More repetitions would provide a clearer estimate for each pair.","The experimental question remains specific: after matching physical distance, does a pair's position relative to the listener's category boundary predict its discriminability?","A better design would still preserve the central matched-distance comparison. Improving measurement should clarify whether crossing the category boundary, rather than another stimulus difference, explains performance."],"speech1-22":["Many acoustic differences do not change a word's identity. Treating every small change as a new linguistic unit would make ordinary variation across repetitions or speakers difficult to handle.","Being worse at hearing some differences can be useful when those differences do not change the linguistic message. Repeated productions of one word are never physically identical in every respect.","A category groups different tokens together so that later processes can access a stable phoneme or word representation. At a boundary, sensitivity is useful because the difference may distinguish words.","Grouping them into a category lets later processes treat them as instances of the same unit. This reduces the number of separate acoustic patterns that word recognition must handle.","The advantage is selective stability: similar treatment within a category and greater differentiation between categories. This explains why being worse at some physical discriminations can accompany successful language recognition.","The useful loss is selective. Differences relevant to a phoneme contrast remain informative, while incidental within-category variation is less likely to create a false change in identity."],"speech1-23":["Ganong's work asks whether known words affect the identity assigned to an ambiguous speech sound. The later speech-perception material examines this lexical influence and models that implement it.","The Ganong reading connects category identification with word knowledge. A listener can use the difference between a real word and a nonword when an initial consonant is ambiguous.","McClelland and colleagues motivate an interactive account in which information can flow between representational levels. The assigned readings connect phoneme categorization to the larger word-recognition system.","The interactive-processing reading examines how activity moves between levels of representation. Features, phonemes, and words can be considered as connected processing units rather than isolated finished steps.","These readings prepare two separate questions: whether partial information continues forward, and whether lexical information returns to influence speech-sound interpretation."],"speech1-24":["Infants cannot press a labeled response button, so researchers use changes in behavior. A **conditioned head turn** rewards turning when a sound changes; **high-amplitude sucking** measures sucking responses linked to sound presentation.","A conditioned head turn turns a detected change into an observable orienting response. The infant learns that a relevant auditory event is associated with a rewarding visual event.","Repeated exposure usually reduces responding. If a new sound restores responding, the infant has detected a change rather than simply continued hearing an indistinguishable repetition.","In high-amplitude sucking, repeated auditory exposure is followed through the infant's sucking behavior. After a response declines, recovery can indicate that a newly presented sound was discriminated.","These methods test discrimination before the infant can name a phoneme. The displayed sucking photograph illustrates the method; it is not itself the Eimas experimental result.","The methods make different infant behaviors serve the same inferential purpose: determining whether the infant notices the specified contrast without asking for a spoken label."],"speech1-25":["Eimas and colleagues repeatedly presented speech sounds to infants and measured sucking. After responding declined, the sound either stayed the same or shifted within or between adult-defined categories.","Repeated presentation first establishes a response that can decline through habituation. The subsequent comparison distinguishes no change, a within-category change, and a between-category change.","A boundary-crossing change produced renewed sucking more clearly than an equally sized within-category change. The response follows perceived category structure rather than physical distance alone.","The physically matched changes are essential. More recovery for a boundary-crossing shift cannot then be attributed simply to that shift having a larger programmed VOT difference.","Infants therefore show categorical-like discrimination before fluent speech production. Early appearance made the phenomenon important to theories of how language abilities develop.","The finding places category-related discrimination early in development. What infants can detect is measured directly through their behavior, even though they cannot explain the contrast or produce the words."],"speech1-26":["Young English-learning infants discriminate some non-English speech contrasts. With development, discrimination of those contrasts declines if they are not maintained by the language environment.","Perceptual narrowing means that previously detectable distinctions become less available when they are not supported by the native-language environment. The change is specific to the contrasts being tested.","The comparison with listeners who hear the contrasts in their language ties the change to experience. The infant system becomes better aligned with distinctions useful in its surroundings.","In the Werker comparison, younger English-learning infants perform better on certain Hindi and Salish contrasts than older English-learning infants. Listeners with relevant language experience preserve a different pattern of sensitivity.","**Perceptual narrowing** is a developmental change in which contrasts remain salient. It is not the claim that infants stop hearing sound or that all possible contrasts were equally detectable initially.","The developmental direction matters: these data show a loss in a nonnative distinction. Other experiments ask whether experience can also produce a distinction that was not initially clear."],"speech1-27":["The proposed narrative begins with broad sensitivity to speech contrasts, followed by tuning to the native language. Learning selects which distinctions continue to organize perception.","The citizens of the world description proposes broadly available speech contrasts at the outset. Native-language experience then tunes which contrasts remain useful.","In this account, categorical speech perception is treated as an early, specialized foundation. The environment narrows an initially broad inventory rather than building every contrast from scratch.","In that account, learning largely selects among possibilities already supplied by the language system. It does not have to construct every perceptual distinction from an undifferentiated auditory input.","This explanation combines an early capacity with experience-dependent change. Its claims about specialization and universality can be tested separately using nonspeech sounds, other animals, and difficult infant contrasts.","Its component claims can be examined separately. Early category behavior, restriction to speech, restriction to humans, and access to every contrast are different propositions requiring different evidence."],"speech1-28":["Categorical perception occurs for nonspeech sounds and in nonhuman animals. Its appearance therefore does not automatically identify a mechanism unique to human language.","Not limited to language concerns the kind of stimulus; not limited to humans concerns the kind of listener. Evidence against one restriction does not by itself test the other.","Final category decisions can also depend on the response task. A sharply divided label curve does not necessarily mean all earlier acoustic processing has become all-or-none.","Task dependence concerns the response being measured. A forced choice can produce a sharper division than a measure that preserves graded uncertainty during recognition.","Infant perception is likewise not uniform across every contrast. Some distinctions need experience to become detectable, adding category formation to the story of developmental tuning.","Not actually universal concerns the infant's starting sensitivity across different contrasts. Together these distinctions motivate a developmental explanation that includes broader perception and learning, rather than one all-purpose inference from early categories."],"speech1-29":["Listeners heard sounds varying along an acoustic continuum associated with plucked and bowed qualities. They labeled the sounds and discriminated pairs at matched physical separations.","The nonspeech continuum varies a sound's onset qualities between plucked and bowed impressions. Adults assign a category and also discriminate pairs drawn from different parts of the continuum.","Labels changed near a boundary, and discrimination was better for pairs crossing that boundary. Sounds on the same side were harder to tell apart despite comparable physical differences.","The key comparison is matched physical separation within versus across the labeling boundary. A peak in discrimination near the category transition reproduces the structure of the speech result.","The same labeling–discrimination relationship can therefore arise without phonemes. Categorical perception describes a perceptual organization that extends beyond speech.","The implication concerns stimulus specificity. An auditory system can organize a continuous nonspeech dimension categorically, so that pattern is not reserved exclusively for phonemes."],"speech1-30":["Infants heard repeated nonspeech sounds while sucking was measured. After habituation, the sound stayed unchanged, changed within a category, or crossed a category boundary.","The high-amplitude sucking procedure distinguishes no change, within categories, and between categories. Habituation supplies the starting point before the sound shift.","Sucking recovered for the between-category shift more than for a within-category shift or unchanged sound. The infant detected the acoustically different sound when it crossed the perceptual boundary.","A within-category acoustic change can produce little recovery, resembling the unchanged condition. A between-category change restores responding more strongly.","Early categorical discrimination therefore extends beyond speech too. Infancy and categorical behavior together do not establish that the underlying capacity is language-specific.","The combination matters: the listener is an infant and the stimuli are nonspeech. Early onset of the behavior therefore does not establish a mechanism dedicated only to human language."],"speech1-31":["Chinchillas were trained with endpoints of a voice-onset-time continuum, using a response associated with food. An **SD**, or discriminative stimulus, signals when the trained response will be rewarded.","Either an endpoint with short VOT or one with long VOT served as a discriminative stimulus for food. The animal learned which response was rewarded in that training situation.","Tests with intermediate speech sounds produced category-like changes in responding. The human comparison also shows a sharp change across the continuum.","Intermediate tokens then tested how the trained responses generalized along the continuum. The comparison with humans concerns the shape of that categorization function.","A nonhuman auditory system can therefore organize these speech stimuli categorically. This challenges the claim that the phenomenon requires possession of human language.","A similar function in an animal without language demonstrates that the auditory pattern can arise outside human language use. It does not mean the animal has acquired the meanings of the human words."],"speech1-32":["The nasal contrast /ma/–/na/ is easier for young infants than /na/–/ŋa/. These sounds differ in place of articulation, or where the constriction is made.","The /na/–/ŋa/ pair changes the place of a nasal constriction at syllable onset. It is initially less salient than /ma/–/na/, so the contrasts cannot be treated as equally easy starting points.","Narayan and colleagues found that the difficult contrast becomes more discriminable with relevant language experience. Filipino-learning infants improve across the ages shown.","The infant response is compared across same and change trials. Relevant later experience increases the difference between those conditions for the difficult contrast.","Development can increase sensitivity as well as reduce it. Learning is therefore more than eliminating unused contrasts from a completely universal starting set.","This creates a gain rather than merely a preserved ability. A complete account of perceptual development must explain both the contrasts that narrow and those that become more sharply differentiated through learning."],"speech2-01":["Spoken-word recognition connects changing acoustic features, phonemes, and stored words. The **lexical–phonetic interface** is the interaction between speech-sound processing and word knowledge.","A word is not heard as a completed symbolic object all at once. Its acoustic evidence arrives over time, and several possible words can fit the beginning.","The main distinctions are when partial information moves forward and whether word-level information feeds back. Cascading and feedback answer these different questions.","The issue is how information crosses the boundary between these representational levels. A stable final word response can result from a process with substantial uncertainty along the way.","Two questions guide the explanation: when does partial evidence reach the next level, and can lexical knowledge influence an earlier phoneme interpretation?"],"speech2-02":["The model passes the waveform through auditory processing, acoustic-property detection, phonetic features, segmental analysis, and lexical search. Each level expresses the signal in a different representational form.","The peripheral auditory system performs spectral analysis, followed by acoustic-property detectors, phonetic-feature detectors, segmental analysis, and lexical search. Each name identifies a different description of the input.","A simple staged interpretation has phoneme identification finish before words can be recognized. Some acoustic detail can be lost as the system converts continuous variation into discrete units.","Detector outputs versus time describe changing acoustic evidence; feature probabilities versus time describe the changing support for features such as voicing. Segmental analysis organizes that evidence into phoneme-related structure.","**Cascaded processing** instead permits partial activation to reach later levels. **Feedback** permits later levels to influence earlier ones; neither term merely means that processing is fast.","Cascaded processing challenges the requirement that one level finish before another begins. Feedback challenges the assumption that influence travels only upward from sound toward words."],"speech2-03":["Beach and peach differ at the initial stop consonant. Changing **voice onset time**, the delay between release and voicing, creates intermediate sounds along a /b/–/p/ continuum.","Voice onset time changes the interval between stop release and vocal-fold vibration. A longer delay supports the voiceless /p/ endpoint, while a shorter delay supports the voiced /b/ endpoint.","Identification changes from one word to the other, while discrimination is strongest around the boundary. These final judgments reproduce the familiar categorical pattern.","The remainder of beach and peach is similar, making the initial voicing difference the crucial manipulation. The spectrograms let that timing be related to the continuum rather than to different word meanings.","The next question concerns what happens before the final choice. Sounds receiving the same word label might still activate competing words by different amounts.","Identification locates the abrupt change in word responses. Discrimination compares token pairs separated by the same number of continuum steps, revealing where those changes are most noticeable."],"speech2-04":["In a **threshold model**, activity is only passed forward from one level to the next when an activity threshold is crossed. A threshold is the activation level that has to be reached before the next stage receives a signal.","The lower feature level can contain changing evidence about whether a sound is voiced. Its activity can rise or fall while the higher phoneme level remains inactive, because the required threshold has not yet been reached.","Once the threshold is crossed, the phoneme signal changes abruptly. The output acts like a gate: later processing receives the selected category, such as /b/, instead of a continuously changing account of the earlier acoustic uncertainty.","Two physically different sounds could therefore produce the same phoneme output. If both cross the threshold for /b/, the word-recognition stage receives the same category even when one sound was closer to the /b/–/p/ boundary.","This produces a prediction about word recognition. Within-category acoustic differences should stop influencing lexical competition once they have been converted into the same phoneme signal. Comparing those tokens tests whether that earlier uncertainty actually survives into the next level."],"speech2-05":["In a **cascaded model**, activity is passed forward continuously over time from one level to the next. The higher level begins using partial information while the lower level is still processing the sound.","Changing feature evidence can therefore change phoneme activity gradually. If the evidence increasingly favors a voiced sound, support for /b/ can rise while some support for the competing /p/ interpretation remains.","That partial phoneme activity can influence words immediately. A token that favors /b/ can activate bear most strongly and still activate pear to a smaller degree. Moving the token closer to the category boundary should increase support for the competing word.","The listener can still give a categorical final response. A task requiring a choice between bear and pear ends with one click, even if both candidates participated before the decision.","Categorical perception might happen later on, or partly reflect the task structure. The useful question is therefore whether graded information reaches words during processing. Eye movements toward the competing picture provide a way to measure that information before the final choice."],"speech2-06":["The lumber mill takes trees and produces lumber; the factory takes lumber and produces a furniture kit; the customer assembles that kit. Each stage requires the previous stage's transformed output.","The three transformations are trees into lumber, lumber into an unassembled furniture kit, and that kit into furniture. The materials required by the next layer have to exist before its work can proceed.","A factory cannot assemble a shipment that has not arrived. Similarly, a strongly staged language model waits for the required earlier representation before beginning the next operation.","The analogy is about a specific kind of dependence, not simply a sequence in time. A later stage accepts only the transformed product it is designed to use.","The analogy captures dependence on completed inputs. It explains what thresholded transmission would mean for the timing of phoneme and word recognition.","For speech, the corresponding prediction is that words wait for phonemic input. They do not receive an ongoing account of how uncertain the earlier acoustic analysis remains."],"speech2-07":["The important feature of the furniture chain is its input requirement. A later layer accepts a particular finished representation, not every partial state of the preceding layer.","Each layer can only take a specific input that has been transformed completely by the previous layer. Waiting for the shipment is the causal feature of the comparison.","Translated to speech, lexical processing would wait for phoneme-level output. Unresolved acoustic uncertainty would be handled upstream before lexical search begins.","If recognition works this way, information that changes only before the phoneme shipment is completed should not directly alter the next stage. The packaged output defines what later processing can use.","Testing this account requires a measure during recognition. A final mouse click alone could hide uncertainty that was active earlier.","The experimental challenge is to look before the final response. A categorical answer by itself cannot establish whether this waiting requirement actually held."],"speech2-08":["In a **food web**, a change in one population affects others through ongoing connections. A drought alters plants, which influences consumers and then predators while the system continues operating.","A drought reducing plants can affect kangaroo rats and then snakes while all populations are still changing. The downstream level responds to a partial change rather than waiting for a final state.","Cascaded processing has the same continuous character: partially changed activation at one level changes the input to other levels immediately rather than after a finished package is delivered.","Adding hawks also changes animals lower in the web. Those reciprocal dependencies illustrate feedback in addition to ongoing forward influence.","Feedback adds influence in the reverse direction. The analogy separates continuous forward propagation from the additional possibility of reciprocal interactions.","In speech, cascading concerns partial evidence reaching words; feedback concerns words influencing phonemes. The food web helps explain why these are related but distinct processing properties."],"speech2-09":["Both accounts can produce a final bear or pear response. The decisive comparison asks whether fine acoustic changes within one response category affect competition before that response.","Thresholded and cascaded systems can agree about which picture is clicked at the end. Their difference appears in whether uncertainty continues to affect the candidates during the decision.","A visual-world task records looks to pictures while a spoken target unfolds. Looking toward the alternative picture provides a time-sensitive index of its involvement in recognition.","An intermediate sound can activate bear most strongly and still leave some support for pear. Measuring that competing support is more informative than merely counting correct clicks.","If competitor looks grow gradually near the category boundary, information has affected lexical processing without having to change the final category decision.","The visual-world method turns the competing interpretation into an observable place to look. A time course can then be compared across acoustically different tokens with the same final response."],"speech2-10":["**McMurray and colleagues** ask whether phoneme identification for word recognition is more like thresholded processing, the lumber-mill analogy, or cascaded processing, the food-web analogy. The issue is whether words receive only a completed phoneme decision or also its developing uncertainty.","They tested identification of a **/b/–/p/ continuum** in a **visual-world display for eye-tracking**. Voice onset time changes the evidence for the initial consonant, while the pictured alternatives make the possible word interpretations visible.","Participants see a target, a competitor, and filler objects. For bear and pear, the two words form a minimal pair: their initial phoneme differs, while the rest of the word is the same. The participant hears a token and clicks the corresponding picture.","Eye-tracking measures where the participant looks before that click. A glance toward pear during a bear response indicates that the competing word is involved, even though bear eventually wins.","The crucial comparison varies VOT among tokens receiving the same final label. If competitor looks change as those tokens approach the category boundary, the word-recognition system is using information that the final bear-or-pear response does not preserve."],"speech2-11":["Participants clicked the picture matching the spoken token. The identification curve was step-like: one part of the continuum generally led to bear, the other to pear.","A typical step-like identification function divides responses into bear and pear regions. Tokens on one side usually yield the same picture selection even though their VOTs still differ.","This reproduces categorical responding in an ordinary recognition task. It does not yet reveal whether all bear responses were equally certain at every earlier moment.","This is why the eye measurement is necessary. The endpoint labels do not record every degree of support that was present before selection.","Eye movements supply the missing time course. They allow comparison of tokens that end in the same click but differ in the strength of the alternative they briefly activate.","The useful comparison holds the final response category constant and varies acoustic distance from the boundary. Any remaining competitor effect then reveals information hidden within that final category."],"speech2-12":["Tokens nearer the phoneme boundary produced more looks to the competing picture. The final click could remain the same while the moment-by-moment distribution of looks changed.","Ambiguity is gradient: there are more looks to the competitor close to the boundary. The fixation curves show when the competing object attracts attention and how long that attraction continues.","The result means that within-category acoustic differences reached word-level processing. A less prototypical bear token created greater temporary competition from pear.","In bear-response trials, pear is the competitor; in pear-response trials, bear is the competitor. Both sets can be examined without mixing correct target looks with competitor looks.","Recognition is therefore richer than the final categorical label. The eye trace reveals partial activation that a single endpoint response conceals.","Near-boundary tokens keep the losing candidate involved more strongly or for longer. The eye trace therefore preserves acoustic uncertainty after a categorical click has ceased to distinguish those trials."],"speech2-13":["The reorganized graph expresses competitor looking relative to distance from the category boundary. Competitor activity increases as the input approaches that boundary.","The early and late windows summarize different portions of the same fixation time course. The curves are organized on either side of the listener's response boundary.","This comparison focuses on **category goodness**: some members fit a category more clearly than others. They can share a final label while differing in how strongly the alternative remains active.","Within each response category, competitor looks are larger nearer the boundary. The late window makes lingering competition especially visible.","The gradual relationship fits continuous propagation. It is harder to explain by a phoneme gate that sends an identical, fully resolved symbol for every token inside one category.","A response switch at the boundary and a graded trend within each side can coexist. The result is therefore not a choice between every aspect being categorical and no category response ever occurring."],"speech2-14":["A bilabial stop can activate features consistent with both /b/ and /p/ by different amounts. **Bilabial** means produced with the two lips; voicing helps distinguish the competing consonants.","The TRACE architecture represents features, phonemes, and words at different possible positions in time. Units are repeated across the temporal layout because a candidate can begin at different moments.","Graded feature activation influences phoneme activation and then candidate words. A near-boundary token leaves more activity in the competitor than a clearly voiced token does.","A sound can be clearly bilabial and a stop while remaining uncertain in voicing. Shared features support both /b/ and /p/ until the voicing evidence differentiates them more strongly.","The resulting looks track this competition before a decision. The same final word can emerge from different amounts of earlier uncertainty.","With cascading, the relative support continues into lexical candidates. Their changing competition provides a mechanism for the changing allocation of eye movements."],"speech2-15":["Nonwords resembling real words can facilitate related meanings: beace primes war, and ging primes queen. **Semantic priming** means a preceding item facilitates processing of a meaning-related target.","Beace resembles peace, and ging resembles king. The altered prime can therefore activate a real-word representation even though the prime itself is not a standard word.","A near-word input can partially activate peace or king without being a correct spoken instance of that word. Their meanings can then influence the following target.","War is related in meaning to peace, and queen to king. Faster processing of those targets indicates that the partially activated word has consequences at the semantic level.","Partial acoustic evidence can therefore have consequences as far as the lexical and semantic levels. Recognition does not need a perfectly completed phoneme sequence before any downstream activation occurs.","This extends the argument beyond a momentary phoneme decision. Incomplete input can affect lexical meaning without first becoming a perfectly identified, correctly formed word."],"speech2-16":["A threshold that passes only the chosen phoneme loses the differences among tokens assigned to that phoneme. It would give later levels essentially the same category output for those tokens.","A hard threshold would remove the distinctions among tokens that all produce the same phoneme output. Those differences could not then explain a systematic gradient at the word level through that output alone.","McMurray's competitor looks vary gradually even among tokens receiving the same final label. The variation therefore survives into recognition instead of vanishing at a discrete gate.","The evidence instead concerns the number and duration of looks to the other member of the minimal pair. That measure changes before the final picture is selected.","Cascading explains how this can happen: partial activation is continuously transmitted. The evidence concerns the flow of uncertainty, not the disappearance of phoneme categories.","The result favors a route through which partial feature information influences phonemes and words. It does not require abandoning the observed categorical shape of the final identification response."],"speech2-17":["The model includes connections from words back to phonemes. A partially active word can reinforce the phoneme sequence consistent with that word.","Feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer sends influence in the reverse direction to the incoming acoustic analysis. A compatible word can support the phonemes that make up its form.","**Feedback** differs from cascading: cascading describes continuous forward flow; feedback describes information traveling from a later representational level toward an earlier one.","Forward activation can begin from a partially matching sound, then lexical activity can change the balance among ambiguous speech candidates. The interaction develops while more input is arriving.","The two can work together. Acoustic input starts several candidates, and lexical knowledge can then change the balance among their component speech sounds.","This is a mechanism for word effects on phoneme categorization. Cascading explains continuous forward availability, while feedback explains the additional lexical influence on earlier processing."],"speech2-18":["Ganong constructed continua between a real word and a nonword, such as corpse and gorpse. The initial sound was varied in small voice-onset-time steps.","Word/nonword pairs such as corpse/gorpse differ in whether a candidate consonant yields a known word. The initial segment is varied along a controlled voicing continuum.","An ambiguous consonant could be interpreted in a way that produces a known word. A different surrounding sequence could favor the other consonant instead.","In one surrounding sequence the word interpretation favors one category; in another sequence it favors the competing category. This allows lexical context to be compared with acoustic evidence.","Comparing the labeling functions tests a **lexical effect** on phoneme identification. The crucial stimuli are ambiguous enough that word knowledge can affect their interpretation.","The Ganong effect concerns that lexical influence on the labeling boundary. The strongest test uses ambiguous tokens where the acoustic input alone gives neither alternative overwhelming support."],"speech2-19":["**Ganong** investigated how phoneme identification is impacted by word knowledge. Word/nonword pairs such as **corpse/gorpse** let the surrounding sounds favor a real word for one interpretation of an ambiguous initial consonant.","The consonant is varied across the relevant phoneme boundary in small, equally spaced steps. This creates clear endpoint tokens and intermediate tokens for which the acoustic evidence is less decisive.","A **late correction process** proposes that categorical perception functions as usual, but a later reporting stage has a bias to report having heard a word. The word preference is applied after the earlier phoneme decision.","That account makes a prediction about the response curves. A general reporting bias should affect the word response beyond only the most ambiguous point, because the correction belongs to the later response stage.","The competing account changes the categorical boundary itself. Comparing where the lexical-context curves differ therefore helps locate the effect: a broad response bias and a difference concentrated near the boundary describe different ways word knowledge could influence the answer."],"speech2-20":["If word knowledge changes the category boundary, its largest effect should occur for ambiguous sounds near that boundary. Clear endpoint tokens need little lexical help.","Word knowledge can work to shift the categorical boundary. The transition in labeling moves because lexical information changes which interpretation of the ambiguous sound is supported.","The same intermediate acoustic input can receive different labels when one surrounding sequence makes /k/ a word and another makes /g/ a word. The transition point shifts with lexical context.","The lexical effect is strongest for items closest to the category boundary. Those tokens leave enough uncertainty for the surrounding word to contribute useful information.","This is a selective interaction between evidence and knowledge. It does not predict that every clearly heard consonant should be overwritten whenever a word is available.","Clear endpoints need less help from lexical context. A selectively enlarged effect around ambiguity is therefore different from adding the same response bias at every continuum position."],"speech2-21":["The observed curves resemble a change around the category boundary more than a uniform late correction. Lexical context has its greatest influence where the consonant is uncertain.","The results are consistent with the boundary-shift prediction: the lexical-context functions diverge especially where phoneme identification is uncertain. The data describe a change in interpretation, not a physical alteration of the input.","Known words therefore affect how the ambiguous speech sequence is categorized. The shape of the effect is part of the evidence, rather than only the total number of word responses.","Ganong discussed possible guessing processes, while later models implemented feedback between lexical and phoneme representations. Distinguishing a pattern from a proposed implementation keeps the reasoning clear.","Later interactive models implement this with lexical-to-phoneme feedback: activated words strengthen the speech sounds that support them.","In the interactive implementation, partial word activation strengthens compatible sounds. This explains why a known word can influence the category assigned to an ambiguous initial segment."],"speech2-22":["**TRACE** represents interacting features, phonemes, and words over time. An unfolding input activates several words compatible with its early sounds.","The simulation traces changing activation as input unfolds across successive frames. Early sound information leaves several word candidates active instead of waiting for one finished word.","The plots show candidates changing activation rather than appearing only after the entire word is heard. Later input changes how well each candidate fits.","The final sounds provide new evidence, changing which candidate best fits the whole sequence. This affects the activation of both words and their associated phonemes.","A simulation connects a proposed mechanism to a time course. Its changing word and phoneme activity provides a process-level account of lexical effects on an ambiguous initial consonant.","The plotted time course is therefore part of the model's explanation. The proposed connections must produce the observed direction of lexical influence as recognition develops."],"speech2-23":["Early input can partially support plug, blood, or blush. When the final /g/ arrives, it strongly favors plug over the competitors whose endings do not match.","When the final /g/ arrives, plug fits that evidence better than blood or blush. The extra support is specific to the candidate whose ending matches.","Increasing plug activation feeds back to its phonemes. This can strengthen the interpretation of the initial sound that makes the full input a word.","Activity accumulates for plug and feeds back toward the phoneme level. The compatible interpretation of the earlier ambiguous onset receives more activation through that route.","The sequence illustrates interaction across time and levels: later evidence changes word competition, and the winning lexical candidate influences an earlier speech-sound interpretation.","Later information can thus change support for an earlier uncertain sound. The system integrates the unfolding word rather than treating each phoneme decision as permanently insulated once it has begun."],"speech2-24":["TRACE shares principles with the **interactive activation** approach to written-word recognition. Representational levels activate compatible candidates, while competition helps select among alternatives.","The Interactive Activation model links representations at several levels and lets compatible units support one another. Competition among alternatives helps the system settle on a coherent interpretation.","Speech adds an important temporal demand. A spoken word arrives progressively, so candidates must be updated as successive sounds become available.","TRACE carries these principles into speech, where evidence for a word arrives across time. A candidate can gain early support and then lose it as its ending becomes inconsistent with the signal.","The relationship is mechanistic rather than merely visual: reciprocal activation and competitive selection can explain word recognition in different input modalities while respecting their different time courses.","The shared principle is interaction between levels, not identical sensory input. The model must respect the temporal organization of speech while using the same kinds of connected representations."],"speech2-25":["Equal physical differences can remain easier to hear across category boundaries. Neither cascading nor lexical feedback requires that this basic categorical-perception result disappear.","Ambiguity about phoneme identity has an impact on word recognition, and word-level information has an impact on phoneme categorization. These are influences in opposite directions.","What changes is the claim that a word-recognition system waits passively for finished phonemes. Acoustic uncertainty can affect words, and word knowledge can affect ambiguous phonemes.","The continued presence of category-related discrimination does not erase those interactions. A final category can still emerge from a process that retains and exchanges graded evidence.","The **lexical–phonetic interface** is therefore interactive. Stable final categories can emerge from a process that retains graded information and exchanges influence between levels.","The word-recognition system therefore cannot simply sit waiting for phoneme recognition to finish. Its candidates are involved while speech-sound uncertainty is still being resolved."],"speech2-26":["The sentence-processing reading extends the ambiguity question to larger structures. Words can fit more than one grammatical arrangement while a sentence unfolds.","Ambiguity in sentence processing asks how words are linked into larger structural relationships. An incomplete sentence can support more than one grammatical analysis.","The relevant connection is timing: does one process finish before another begins, or do several sources of information constrain interpretation together? This prepares the move from phoneme and word competition to competing sentence analyses.","The connection with speech perception concerns processing order. Does a specialized stage provide a completed analysis, or do other sources of information affect it before completion?","The assigned sentence-processing material develops that comparison through garden paths, world knowledge, and visual context. These concern the interpretation of structures built from the recognized words."],"speech2-27":["As a spoken word begins, words sharing its onset form a **cohort** of candidates. Listeners can look toward both the target and an onset-sharing competitor before the ending separates them.","The cohort effect occurs because the beginning of a word is shared with other possible words. A compatible onset activates those candidates before the final distinguishing sounds arrive.","In the visual-world display, the target gains looks as evidence accumulates. The cohort competitor attracts early looks, then loses support when the rest of the sound no longer matches.","In the visual-world display, an onset-sharing competitor can attract looks early and then decline. A rhyme-related candidate has a different relationship to the unfolding input and can show a different time course.","The eye-movement time course shows recognition operating during the word. The system does not have to wait for a completed acoustic word before considering lexical candidates.","The changing fixation curves show that word recognition is very fast and incremental. Correct final selection can be preceded by several temporarily active alternatives."],"speech2-28":["The simulation and eye traces track changing activity for targets and different competitors. Their peaks and declines reveal when a candidate is temporarily plausible.","Eye movements give an indication of changes in candidate activity over time. Comparing the simulation with those traces tests more than whether the model eventually chooses the right word.","A final correct click compresses that history into one answer. Time-resolved measures preserve the competition that occurred on the way to the answer.","A successful account needs plausible rises and declines for different competitors as evidence changes. The target's final advantage is only one part of that dynamic pattern.","This links cohort effects to graded category goodness: both show information influencing recognition before a final categorical response is issued.","This is the value of observing processing before a final response is issued. Temporal measures can reveal what a categorical endpoint necessarily leaves out."],"aphasias-01":["**Aphasia** is an acquired disruption of language associated with brain injury. Different patterns of speaking and understanding can reveal which operations a language task requires.","Language is not one indivisible action. Producing a word, understanding a sentence, repeating a sound, and assigning event roles can place different demands on the system.","The central comparison is between a loss of linguistic knowledge and difficulty using that knowledge under processing demands. Carefully designed comprehension tasks help separate these explanations.","An acquired impairment means that a previously functioning ability has changed. Comparing what remains effective with what becomes difficult can suggest how those operations are organized.","The explanation needs both a description of behavior and a theory of the operations producing it. The lecture develops that connection through competing accounts of grammar, lexical processing, and broader task demands."],"aphasias-02":["Early neurologists such as Wernicke and Lichtheim described specialized centers joined by pathways. Damage to a connection could disrupt one operation while leaving other operations available.","The centers stand for sound images, movement images, and concepts. In the house diagram, A represents word-sound patterns, M represents movement patterns for speaking, and B represents conceptual information.","In the diagram, comprehension and verbatim repetition can fail through different disconnections. **Repetition** means reproducing what was heard, which is not necessarily identical to understanding its meaning.","An A–B disconnection can leave recognition of the sound pattern without its usual access to meaning. An A–M disconnection instead interrupts the direct route useful for verbatim repetition.","A box-and-arrow model states which representations communicate. This makes distinct predicted impairment patterns possible instead of treating every language difficulty as the same disorder.","The numbered sites make distinct predictions because a center and a connection are not the same thing. The task can fail through loss of a representation or through failure to communicate between still-available representations."],"aphasias-03":["Geschwind revived a connected-system account of brain and language. The diagram places auditory, motor, and connecting regions within an anatomical model.","The anatomical illustration connects Broca's area, Wernicke's area, and the arcuate fasciculus. The fasciculus is a fiber pathway linking regions rather than a third store of word meanings.","By this period, linguistics also distinguished computations such as syntax from lexical knowledge. Clinical differences could therefore be interpreted using more specific candidate functions.","The new linguistic theory treated syntax as independent of other aspects of language, with its own computational principles. That made a selectively grammatical deficit a more precisely stated possibility.","A **double dissociation** compares contrasting patterns of impairment. It can motivate separating operations, but the task definitions determine which operations the comparison actually tests.","A double dissociation compares one pattern of impaired and spared functions with a contrasting pattern in another patient or group. The proposed separation depends on defining the tested functions well enough to interpret the contrast."],"aphasias-04":["Broca-type speech often contains word-finding pauses, omitted grammatical morphemes, and disturbed word order. **Agrammatism** refers to this reduced or disrupted grammatical organization.","Word-finding pauses interrupt the effort to retrieve a word. Grammatical morphemes include small function words and endings that signal relations such as tense or number.","Wernicke-type speech can be fluent and well articulated while containing substitutions, invented words, and comprehension problems. Fluency describes the flow of speech rather than whether its content is accurate.","Fromkin describes Wernicke's patients as producing fluent speech with good intonation and pronunciation, but with substitutions, neologisms, and phonological errors. An orderly speech rhythm is different from accurate word selection.","Everyday conversation may make comprehension seem better than controlled testing shows. The passage also distinguishes behavioral syndromes from a perfectly one-to-one match with a named brain area.","The behavioral label and the lesion label do not map perfectly in both directions. Controlled comprehension tests reveal difficulties that can be masked when conversational context supplies many other clues."],"aphasias-05":["**Telegraphic speech** retains important content words but has difficulty with grammatical particles, inflections, and ordering. The listener may infer an intended message from the surviving words.","The Wernicke-type reply my wires don't hire right preserves a recognizable sentence frame while the selected words fail to express the intended answer clearly. The sound relation between wires and hire illustrates how form can guide an incorrect selection.","Wernicke-type **word salad** can preserve the outward rhythm and rough syntax of speech while word selection and meaningful organization break down. A **neologism** is an invented word form.","The Broca-type hospital narrative retains items such as Dad, hospital, and two doctors amid pauses. Those content words convey part of the event while articles, verbs, and grammatical links are largely missing.","These profiles suggest a contrast between grammatical organization and lexical selection. The contrast motivates experiments rather than replacing the need to test those functions directly.","This contrast is the attractive dichotomy: fluent but semantically disrupted speech on one side, halting and grammatically reduced speech on the other. Syntax roughly intact is a relative behavioral description, not the claim that every sentence is flawless."],"aphasias-06":["Broca's original patients had profound problems producing articulate speech. This historical description is not identical to the later syndrome defined mainly through agrammatic language.","Wernicke's aphasia is interpreted as difficulty with activation or inhibition in the lexicon. Activation makes candidate words available; inhibition helps suppress competitors that should not be selected.","**Apraxia of speech** concerns planning or controlling speech movements. A person may have difficulty producing the movements without having lost all grammatical knowledge.","Broca's original patient Leborgne, known as Tan, could produce very little articulate speech while appearing to understand much of what was said. That is not the same output pattern as a lengthy but agrammatic utterance.","Calling both patterns Broca-related can hide this distinction. The question is whether the observed failure belongs to motor execution, linguistic structure, or a combination.","A modern account must identify whether the difficulty concerns linguistic structure or planning the movements of speech. Apraxia of speech addresses the latter problem, so the historical name should not substitute for a task-based description."],"aphasias-07":["The **lexicon** contains word-level representations; **grammar** organizes combinations and structural relations. The proposed aphasia dichotomy maps Wernicke-type problems onto lexical activation and Broca-type problems onto grammar.","Acquiring language, in the formal account discussed here, involves internalizing a system capable of generating new sentences. Understanding a new sentence depends partly on recovering how that structure was built.","Formal linguistic arguments made this separation plausible by distinguishing grammatical structure from meaning. A sentence can be syntactically organized even when its meaning is strange.","Colorless green ideas sleep furiously has a recognizable English arrangement even though its meaning is anomalous. Reversing those words disrupts the grammatical arrangement without making the meanings more sensible.","The clinical prediction is specific: a grammar impairment should affect tasks that need structural relationships, including comprehension when word meaning alone cannot supply the answer.","The contrast makes syntax and semantics separable dimensions of the stimulus. That distinction gives the clinical grammar–lexicon proposal a more specific theoretical foundation."],"aphasias-08":["The production framework separates selecting lexical items from organizing their grammatical positions and forms. **Lemmas** are lexical representations linked to intended meanings; positional processes arrange grammatical structure.","At the functional level, a speaker selects lexical representations and establishes semantic roles. At the positional level, word forms and grammatical elements are arranged into the structure of the utterance.","The relationship between a word and its referent is often arbitrary. Affixes and structural combinations instead reflect rules governing how pieces are assembled.","HOUSEBOAT and DOGHOUSE invite an objection because their meanings are related to their parts. Word formation must therefore be considered alongside selection of an arbitrary lexical label.","The error easy enoughly illustrates morphology participating in production processes rather than being only a stored whole-word meaning. This is why the grammar–lexicon contrast involves more than long words versus short words.","In easy enoughly, the -ly ending appears on enough rather than on easy. The error makes an affix available as a separate piece in the assembly process, illustrating the role of derivational morphology at the positional level."],"aphasias-09":["A listener can seem to understand a sentence by combining familiar meanings and contextual expectations. This success does not necessarily show that every syntactic dependency was computed.","A sentence can be understood through probable event knowledge even when a particular grammatical dependency is not fully resolved. A familiar noun and verb combination may strongly suggest who usually acts.","An autonomous-parser account proposes structural computations that can be distinguished from meaning. To test them, sentence materials must make structure determine the intended interpretation.","Parsing mechanisms compute structural relationships rather than merely accumulating the meanings of content words. Isolating them requires stimuli where a plausible shortcut and the actual structure do not always give the same answer.","Broca-type patients could therefore follow many conversations while struggling with sentences whose grammatical relationships cannot be recovered from familiar event knowledge.","A patient can therefore follow ordinary conversation while having difficulty in a focused structural test. The difference follows from the information each situation makes available."],"aphasias-10":["Broca's historical observation separated articulate speech from comprehension. A later theory asks a different question: can grammatical processing be impaired across speaking and understanding?","The historical dissociation involved articulate speech and understanding. Later linguistic theory supplied additional distinctions among phonetic production, word representations, and syntactic organization.","A comprehension task reduces the need to produce speech. If difficulty remains when the participant can simply choose a picture, impaired articulation alone is insufficient to explain that response pattern.","To demonstrate a broader problem with syntax, a test must demand structural interpretation without requiring a spoken sentence in response. Selecting a picture is one way to reduce articulation demands.","The best materials isolate who performs an action and who receives it. They force structural information to contribute instead of letting the task be solved entirely through familiar meanings.","The informative failure would be difficulty assigning the roles specified by grammar. Merely showing reduced speech or a hesitation does not target that operation as directly."],"aphasias-11":["**Competence** refers to linguistic knowledge; **performance** is its use under actual processing and response demands. Motor difficulties can change speech output even if some underlying knowledge remains.","We are observing what the individual can do despite the impairment. A speaker may keep essential content words and omit less informative material because speaking has become effortful.","Jackson's point is that the behavior observed after injury is what the person can still accomplish. A short, agrammatic utterance may reflect an adaptation to difficulty rather than a literal picture of an erased grammar.","Competence refers to knowledge, while performance is the use of that knowledge in a real task. Motor difficulty can constrain the output even when the person retains more knowledge than the utterance reveals.","Caramazza and Zurif therefore examined comprehension. The aim was to see whether grammatical relationships cause difficulty when the required response does not depend on producing a fluent sentence.","Comprehension supplies another route to the same structural question. The goal is to see whether role assignment remains difficult when the patient does not have to articulate a full response."],"aphasias-12":["For the dog bit the man, word meanings and familiar event knowledge favor the same interpretation as ordinary English word order. Several routes can lead to a correct response.","Canonical English order often places the agent before the verb and the undergoer after it. An agent performs the action; an undergoer is affected by it.","In the dog that the man is biting is angry, the man performs the biting and the dog receives it. A dog-bites-man expectation gives the wrong answer.","In the dog that the man is biting is angry, the embedded clause gives man the acting role. The first-mentioned dog is the object of biting and the subject of is angry.","This sentence separates **semantic heuristics**, shortcuts based on likely events, from computing the actual relationship. Choosing the matching picture requires tracking the embedded grammatical structure.","This reverses the easy dog-bites-man expectation while preserving recognizable words. A correct response requires using the clause relations instead of allowing familiar world knowledge to choose the event."],"aphasias-13":["The sentence materials include **control**, **semantically constrained center-embedded**, **reversible center-embedded**, and **improbable center-embedded** sentences. The comparison changes how much sentence meaning can help recover the grammatical relationships.","A control provides a relatively straightforward description of an action. **Center embedding** places one clause inside another, so the listener has to connect the nouns with their roles while keeping the larger sentence in mind.","In **“The apple that the boy is eating is red,”** semantic plausibility strongly supports the intended relationship. A boy can eat an apple, so familiar event knowledge helps identify who is acting on what.","In **“The cat that the dog is biting is black,”** either animal could plausibly bite the other. This is reversible: the listener needs the grammatical relationships to select the picture showing the dog biting the cat.","In **“The dog that the man is biting is black,”** the intended action conflicts with the more familiar event of a dog biting a man. Choosing the correct picture requires following the sentence even when ordinary expectations favor the opposite direction. Comparing these conditions shows how grammatical processing and plausibility contribute to comprehension."],"aphasias-14":["Healthy controls performed well across sentence types. Wernicke-type patients were poor more broadly, consistent with substantial comprehension difficulty.","The task asks the patient to choose a picture matching the sentence. A successful response requires linking recognized words to the particular event relation expressed.","Broca-type patients had particular difficulty when structure conflicted with likely roles or when the nouns could plausibly exchange roles. The trouble concerned keeping who did what to whom straight.","Broca's patients were especially vulnerable when semantic roles did not supply the right answer. Recognizing dog, man, and biting was not enough to keep their relationship straight.","This pattern supported the proposal that syntax contributes a distinct operation. It showed why everyday comprehension can conceal problems that become visible in carefully contrasted sentence materials.","The original grammatical interpretation concerns a pattern across conditions. The same noun meanings can be available while the structural route needed for an unusual or reversible relation is less effective."],"aphasias-15":["Broad descriptions such as fluent or poor comprehension summarize behavior but combine many processes. A language theory can suggest finer distinctions, such as lexical access versus structural role assignment.","The grammar versus the lexicon distinction turns a broad clinical observation into a more precise processing question. A lexical problem concerns which words and meanings become available; a structural problem concerns how their relations are computed.","Experiments then make the proposed distinction observable. Sentence–picture matching separates recognizing content words from using the grammar to connect their referents.","The value of a theory is that it suggests contrasts capable of separating those possibilities. Fluency and overall comprehension remain useful descriptions, but they combine several operations.","The resulting structure–function question concerns particular operations rather than a whole faculty called language. The explanatory value comes from linking a defined task contrast to a defined impairment pattern.","A structure–function explanation therefore joins a defined operation to a defined task pattern. The subsequent studies ask whether the implicated operation is used only in language or across other kinds of meaningful behavior."],"aphasias-16":["Participants indicated which animal performed the action. In the dog is biting the cat, the dog is the agent; it remains the agent in it's the cat that the dog is biting.","All four examples describe the dog acting on the cat. What changes is how that relation is expressed: active, subject cleft, object cleft, or passive.","The four forms were active, subject cleft, object cleft, and passive. **Object clefts** and **passives** require interpreting a first-mentioned noun that is not simply the acting participant.","It's the cat that the dog is biting names the affected animal first. The cat is bitten by the dog also places the patient of the action before its agent.","Holding the event relation constant while varying its expression tests sensitivity to sentence form. The target is the agent, not whichever animal happens to be named first.","The instruction is to indicate which animal is doing the action. A first-noun shortcut succeeds on some forms and fails on others, letting the experiment measure the cost of the structural relation."],"aphasias-17":["Broca-type patients struggled particularly with object clefts and passives relative to easier forms. The pattern resembles the earlier difficulty with noncanonical or structurally demanding relationships.","The graphs compare sentence forms within several participant groups. An overall lower curve shows general task difficulty, while a larger drop for object clefts and passives shows an additional structural cost.","The participant must map the sentence onto the correct actor while resisting a first-noun-as-agent shortcut. That shortcut works for many active sentences but fails for an object cleft.","Noncanonical forms require more than simply matching the first animal to the actor. The listener has to preserve the grammatical cue linking the later noun to the action.","The graph compares performance patterns across groups rather than diagnosing grammar from one missed item. Both overall accuracy and the additional cost of particular forms matter.","That selective cost resembles the earlier role-assignment finding. The next manipulation asks whether a similar profile can emerge when a healthy system has fewer processing resources available."],"aphasias-18":["Healthy undergraduates also became especially vulnerable on object clefts and passives when speech was accelerated or a secondary memory task added demands.","Speeding up speech leaves less time for the evolving analysis. Remembering a list of numbers adds a second task that competes for resources during comprehension.","The manipulation changes available processing resources rather than giving the participants a brain lesion. The resulting selective difficulty resembles part of the patient pattern.","Object clefts and passives are especially vulnerable under these stresses. Their difficulty appears in participants without the focal clinical injury used to define the patient group.","A structurally selective deficit can therefore emerge through **performance** demands. The shared pattern raises the possibility that demanding sentences expose a more general processing bottleneck.","This makes a performance explanation concrete: demanding sentence forms expose a bottleneck when resources are reduced. Structural selectivity can arise from the interaction between the task and a strained processing system."],"aphasias-19":["Patients matched an action to an object using linguistic and nonlinguistic stimuli. The drawings make meaningful relations available without requiring a sentence to carry all the information.","An action can be represented with a verbal description or a meaningful gesture or pantomime. Pantomime depicts an action without supplying the same spoken grammatical sequence.","Broca-type patients had difficulty in both versions. Removing language did not eliminate the relevant task difficulty.","If the task is to match the action to the object, both formats still require the participant to recover a meaningful relation. The linguistic status of the input is what changes.","The result connects linguistic action interpretation with broader action or event processing. It tests whether the impairment is confined to sentence grammar by preserving the relation while changing its presentation.","Difficulty in both versions points toward a contribution shared with action understanding. The comparison is informative because removing the sentence does not remove the relevant event relation."],"aphasias-20":["Performance on the nonverbal task was associated with damage involving Broca's area. The anatomical relationship therefore extended beyond a language-only version of the task.","The lesion analysis links difficulty interpreting nonverbal actions with damage involving Broca's area. A nonverbal task can therefore produce an anatomical relationship similar to one seen in language work.","A **lesion–behavior association** relates the location of damaged tissue to measured performance. Here the behavior involves interpreting or matching an action relation without a spoken sentence.","Lesion correlates connect variation in damaged tissue with variation in behavior across patients. They are not simply a picture of where the task appears active in one healthy person.","The evidence motivates a functional description broader than grammar alone. A region can contribute to language tasks because those tasks also depend on operations used outside language.","The relevant functional description must fit both task types. A region involved in organizing or understanding action relations can contribute to sentence comprehension without its entire role being exhausted by grammar."],"aphasias-21":["Patients matched environmental sounds to pictures and also completed a verbal version. An environmental sound conveys information about an event or object without being a word.","Environmental sounds such as an animal call can be matched to the corresponding picture without a spoken noun as the target. A verbal condition presents the linguistic label instead.","Wernicke-type patients were particularly poor, and the reported verbal–nonverbal difference was not significant. Their difficulty extended to extracting meaning from sounds outside speech.","Both conditions connect an auditory pattern with meaning. Wernicke's patients were particularly impaired, with no significant difference reported between the verbal and nonverbal versions.","The comparison preserves auditory meaning matching while changing whether the input is linguistic. This helps explain why poor spoken comprehension does not necessarily identify a language-exclusive deficit.","The result places auditory comprehension in a broader context. The task contrast asks whether language is the exclusive locus of difficulty or one use of an affected meaning-related auditory system."],"aphasias-22":["The lesion maps relate poor sound–picture matching to damage involving Wernicke's area. Associations are shown for both verbal and nonverbal inputs.","The overlays show where damage is associated with impairments in verbal and nonverbal sound–picture matching. The overlap involves the posterior region traditionally associated with Wernicke's aphasia.","The overlap matters because both tasks require connecting an auditory pattern with an appropriate meaning. One uses words; the other uses meaningful environmental sounds.","Because the nonverbal stimuli still require meaningful auditory recognition, the comparison does not reduce comprehension to speech alone. Both formats must reach an appropriate pictured referent.","The anatomical result therefore fits a contribution to auditory comprehension broader than word labels alone. Behavioral task comparisons and lesion maps address the same functional question from different directions.","Together with the behavioral data, the maps support a shared contribution to these tasks. The explanatory unit is the processing demand made visible by the comparison, rather than a syndrome name by itself."],"aphasias-23":["A grammar–lexicon account predicts dissociable linguistic operations. A broader processing account asks whether similar difficulties occur when comparable demands are imposed without language.","The debate now uses sophisticated tests, quantitative behavior, and quantitative structure–function analyses. These permit more specific questions than whether a patient is generally fluent or understands conversation.","Controlled sentences, action matching, and environmental-sound matching give these proposals measurable predictions. Accuracy patterns are compared across conditions and related quantitatively to lesions.","A grammar-specific proposal must explain the sentence contrasts. A broader processing proposal must also explain why the same vulnerability can arise under stress or with nonverbal stimuli.","The resulting debate concerns what a region or impairment contributes to a task. Naming a syndrome is the starting point for explaining the behavior, not the entire explanation.","The evidence becomes useful by linking a prediction to a measured pattern. Carefully changing the task can show which parts of the proposed function survive the change in input or response format."],"aphasias-24":["The next topic asks how language functions emerge rather than how they change after injury. Developmental evidence can help identify the contributions of experience and initial learning capacities.","Development asks what initial capacities and learning experiences build the system before fluent language is established. Aphasia asks how an already developed system performs after disruption.","The connection is functional: successful language depends on several interacting operations. Their appearance, change, and vulnerability can be studied through carefully chosen behavioral tasks.","Both approaches need a task that makes a particular operation visible. An infant's looking or sucking and a patient's picture choice are indirect responses to a defined information-processing demand.","The transition therefore preserves the experimental logic while changing the population. The next material uses early speech discrimination to reason about the origins of linguistic categories."],"aphasias-25":["**Voxel-based lesion–symptom mapping** compares damage at small brain locations with differences in behavior across many patients. A voxel is a small three-dimensional unit in a brain image.","Voxel-based lesion–symptom mapping formalizes the comparison across many patients. At each small image location, the analysis relates damage to the measured symptom.","The displayed maps associate broad fluency deficits more with anterior regions and broad comprehension deficits more with posterior regions. This supports a rough production–perception separation at that level of description.","Fluency deficits and comprehension deficits are broad categories in these figures. Their different anterior and posterior associations support a rough double dissociation at that level of description.","The particular symptom measure determines the interpretation. Broad fluency maps and precise action-matching contrasts ask related but differently detailed questions about brain function.","A map is interpreted in relation to its symptom measure. More detailed grammatical or lexical questions require behavioral measures that isolate those processes, just as the sentence and sound-matching studies do."],"development1-01":["Language development asks how an infant's changing abilities arise from initial capacities and experience. Speech perception is especially useful because it can be measured before fluent speaking.","Speech perception offers a way to study learning before a child can explain or name the categories. An observable response can reveal whether the infant detects a particular sound change.","The lecture moves from categorical perception to learning from sound distributions and social interaction. The common question is what experience changes in the perceptual system.","The theoretical comparison concerns what the child brings to the task and what the environment contributes. Early presence, statistical tuning, and social interaction address different pieces of that explanation.","The later experiments therefore build on the categorical-perception result rather than merely repeating it. They ask which initial sensitivities change, what exposure causes, and how the context of exposure matters."],"development1-02":["The question of **nature and nurture in child language** asks what the child brings to acquisition and what is learned from experience. The **nativist positions** discussed here grow out of Chomsky’s critique of Skinner. Skinner’s behaviorist approach studied general learning mechanisms, including reward and punishment, and the critique asks whether those mechanisms can explain the linguistic system children acquire.","For Chomsky, **the important feature of language is its generativity**. Generativity means that speakers can produce and understand new sentences rather than only repeating sentences already encountered. A language is therefore not just a stored list of familiar utterances. Its organization makes new combinations possible.","From this perspective, **acquiring a language means identifying the abstract grammar that produces sentences in the language**. The grammar is the underlying system that determines how expressions can be structured. The child has to work out that system from the language encountered, so that the resulting knowledge extends beyond the particular examples heard.","According to the nativist argument, the general learning mechanisms studied by behaviorists were not up to this task, and there are two reasons for that claim. First, **grammatical and ungrammatical sentences are not “similar” in any straightforward way**. A well-formed and an ill-formed sentence can look very similar, while two well-formed sentences can look quite different. Surface resemblance alone therefore does not tell the learner which structural possibilities belong to the language.","Second, **the context for language acquisition does not look like operant conditioning: reward/punishment contingencies do not hold**. In operant conditioning, a behavior becomes more or less frequent depending on its consequences. Children are not systematically rewarded for grammatical sentences and punished for ungrammatical ones. The particular consequence pattern required by that explanation is therefore not consistently available to teach the grammar.","Instead, nativist approaches assert **innate knowledge of what language is like, or Universal Grammar**, and **language-specific acquisition processes**. These proposals concern both the possible structures a child considers and the processes used to acquire them. Children’s rapid acquisition of broadly comparable, complex grammars motivates the claim that humans have a special **hypothesis-formulating ability**: a capacity for working out the hidden organization of the language around them."],"development1-03":["Categorical perception makes equal physical differences unequal perceptually. Sounds crossing a phoneme boundary are distinguished more easily than sounds within one category.","Similarity between stimuli is defined in linguistic rather than physical terms in the categorical pattern. Two equal acoustic steps can differ in how easily listeners notice them.","Early theories interpreted this as an auditory signal being translated into language-relevant symbols. Its apparent restriction to humans and speech made a specialized mechanism seem plausible.","The original specialization argument combined an early behavior with the idea that it appeared only for speech and only in humans. Those components need to be kept separate when reading the later evidence.","The key distinction is between describing the perceptual pattern and explaining its origin. Similarity defined by a category can emerge through different kinds of learning or initial organization.","A category-related discrimination curve describes what perception does. Determining whether its mechanism is innate, learned, or shared with other domains requires additional comparisons."],"development1-04":["A conditioned head turn rewards an infant for turning when a sound changes. High-amplitude sucking instead measures sucking linked to presentation of the auditory stimulus.","A head turn is conditioned so that the infant's response to a detected auditory event can be observed. A visual reward helps make the change behaviorally relevant.","Repetition causes **habituation**, a decline in response. A renewed response after a sound change indicates that the infant detected a difference.","High-amplitude sucking follows a different response: repeated presentation leads to declining sucking, and a new sound may restore it. Recovery is compared with an unchanged condition.","The measure supplies evidence without asking for a verbal category label. The comparison must specify whether the sound stayed the same, changed within a category, or crossed a boundary.","Neither method asks an infant to pronounce or explicitly label a phoneme. They measure sensitivity through behavior the infant can already perform."],"development1-05":["Infants heard repeated speech tokens varying in **voice onset time**, the interval between stop release and the onset of voicing. Sucking was measured before and after a stimulus shift.","Voice onset time is the delay between a consonant release and the beginning of vocal-fold vibration. The experiment compares selected changes along that acoustic dimension.","A between-category shift produced stronger recovery than a matched within-category shift. An unchanged condition supplied a comparison for responding without a new sound.","In the no-change condition, repetition continues. Within- and between-category conditions introduce physical changes whose different category relationships are the important contrast.","The result is categorical-like discrimination early in life. Infants distinguished the changes according to their relation to a speech boundary, rather than responding equally to every physical shift.","Renewed sucking is greater for the category-crossing change. The result connects an infant discrimination response with a boundary that also organizes adult speech categorization."],"development1-06":["At around four months, many motor and visual skills are still developing, and speech production is only beginning to resemble vowel-like sounds. Categorical discrimination appears early relative to those milestones.","The infants were still developing basic visual and motor skills, and were only beginning vowel-like vocalizations. The speech-perception finding therefore appeared strikingly early relative to fluent production.","If the ability were unique to speech and humans, its early emergence would fit an account of an innate language specialization. This made the Eimas finding influential.","The argument was that an apparently language-specific ability emerging at that age might be part of the initial organization supporting acquisition. That is why the result mattered for theories of innateness.","Early presence establishes when a behavior can be observed. The claims that it is language-specific or independent of learning require additional comparisons, which the following experiments provide.","The same reasoning also identifies the next tests. If the pattern appears outside speech or outside humans, its early presence remains real while the proposed specialization must be reconsidered."],"development1-07":["Young English-learning infants detect contrasts that distinguish sounds in Hindi or Salish even though those contrasts are not used in English. Older English-learning infants show reduced discrimination.","At around six months, English-learning infants can discriminate some contrasts they have never heard in their own language environment. Their later sensitivity to certain nonnative contrasts declines.","Infants remain sensitive to distinctions supported by their own linguistic environment. Experience changes which differences organize perception most effectively.","The Hindi and Salish comparisons ask how experience changes particular distinctions. Relevant native input supports a different outcome from an English environment where those distinctions are not needed.","**Perceptual narrowing** describes this increasing tuning. The loss concerns particular distinctions; it is not a general decline in hearing or the ability to learn any new information.","This is tuning of the perceptual organization. A contrast can become less available even as the child becomes more effective at recognizing the words and sounds used in the surrounding language."],"development1-08":["The proposed account starts with many available phonetic contrasts. Exposure to the native language selects a smaller set that remains useful for distinguishing its words.","Children start off as citizens of the world in this proposed narrative. It attributes broad initial access to phonetic contrasts before native-language specialization.","Learning is therefore described as choosing among innately available categories. A broad initial capacity becomes specialized through the child's environment.","Learning selects among many possible innately specified contrasts and narrows the inventory to those meaningful in the native language. That is a specific account of what experience is doing.","This narrative makes concrete predictions: categorical perception should be special to language, and infants should begin with access to the relevant contrasts. Both claims can be tested independently.","Evidence of an experience-created distinction would demand more than selection from an already complete set. The upcoming contrast studies therefore address the claimed initial inventory as well as its later tuning."],"development1-09":["Categorical patterns also occur with nonspeech sounds, nonhuman listeners, and different task demands. The same outward behavior does not always identify a uniquely linguistic mechanism.","Not limited to language is tested with nonspeech sounds. Not limited to humans is tested with other species responding to speech stimuli. These are different comparisons.","Infant contrasts differ in initial salience, meaning how easy they are to detect. Some improve with relevant experience instead of being present early and then lost.","Task-dependent behavior means that the response procedure can affect how sharply categories appear. A final forced choice may hide the graded evidence available earlier.","A developmental explanation therefore needs both category tuning and category formation. It must explain why experience can strengthen some distinctions while reducing sensitivity to others.","Not actually universal concerns differences between infant contrasts. If a difficult contrast becomes salient with input, learning is contributing to the formation of useful distinctions, not merely deleting some of them."],"development1-10":["Adults labeled nonspeech sounds with plucked or bowed qualities and discriminated equally separated pairs. The sounds varied in an acoustic property such as their onset characteristics.","The sounds vary between plucked and bowed qualities. Listeners give labels along the continuum and then discriminate pairs with the same physical step separation.","Labeling changed around a category boundary, and discrimination was best near that boundary. The physically matched within-category changes were less noticeable.","The label transition and discrimination advantage occur in corresponding parts of the continuum. Within-category pairs are less distinguishable than equally spaced boundary-crossing pairs.","Categorical perception can therefore organize nonspeech input. Its signature is the relationship between category labeling and discrimination, not simply the presence of spoken syllables.","This reproduces the categorical-perception relationship with nonspeech input. Its existence therefore identifies a form of perceptual organization that does not have to be tied to a phoneme inventory."],"development1-11":["Infants first heard a repeated nonspeech sound until sucking declined. The stimulus then remained unchanged, shifted within a category, or crossed a boundary.","The infant hears a repeated nonspeech sound until responding has declined. After the shift, the sound either remains the same, changes within a category, or changes between categories.","Renewed sucking appeared most clearly for the between-category condition. The within-category condition resembled the unchanged condition more closely despite a physical change.","The between-category condition produces recovery, while the within-category change produces much less. The unchanged group shows what continuing habituation looks like without a new auditory event.","Infants can therefore show the categorical pattern outside speech. The result connects early auditory organization with a broader perceptual capacity.","This extends the adult nonspeech comparison into infancy. It directly separates the early age of the response from a requirement that the sound be linguistic."],"development1-12":["The **chinchilla experiment** asks whether speech categorization depends on possessing human language. The animals learn a rewarded response to endpoint speech stimuli and are then tested on sounds between those endpoints.","A short-lag or long-lag voice-onset-time endpoint serves as an **SD**, a discriminative stimulus signaling that a response can receive food. The endpoint is therefore meaningful to the animal through the training contingency.","Once that response has been learned, intermediate VOT tokens test generalization. The question is which trained response each unfamiliar token produces as its timing moves along the continuum.","The resulting response curve changes in a category-like way, broadly resembling human labeling. Equal physical steps along the acoustic dimension can therefore produce a sharper change between response categories.","Human speech can use an auditory organization also found in an animal without human language. This challenges the claim that the categorical pattern itself requires a uniquely human, language-specific mechanism, and supports considering more general perceptual capacities."],"development1-13":["Young infants detected /ma/–/na/ more readily than /na/–/ŋa/. The latter distinguishes two nasal places of articulation at the beginning of a syllable.","Both contrasts involve nasal consonants, but /na/–/ŋa/ changes a less readily detected place distinction. The easier /ma/–/na/ contrast produces a clearer same-versus-change response in young infants.","Looking behavior changed when an easy contrast switched, but not comparably for the difficult contrast. The same method therefore reveals unequal sensitivity to different speech distinctions.","Salience means perceptual prominence or detectability. The different response patterns show that the physical contrasts do not all start with equivalent behavioral accessibility.","The initial state is not a universal inventory in which every possible contrast is equally available. Acoustic salience is part of what the learning system begins with.","A universal starting inventory would treat the relevant distinctions as already available. Unequal initial detectability makes the role of later experience especially important for the harder contrast."],"development1-14":["Filipino-learning infants improved on /na/–/ŋa/ between the younger and older ages shown. Their environment provides information that makes this contrast linguistically relevant.","The older Filipino-learning infants show greater sensitivity to a contrast used in their language environment. Looking is compared when the syllable repeats and when it changes.","Older infants showed a clearer behavioral difference between same and change trials. The ability grows with experience instead of merely surviving from an equally sensitive initial state.","Greater looking in the change condition indicates that the switch has become behaviorally effective. This is a developmental gain in detecting the distinction.","Learning can therefore add effective distinctions. This complements perceptual narrowing: development changes the organization of perception in both directions.","Exposure to contrasts in the environment is necessary for acquisition in some cases. Learning therefore includes increasing the salience of a distinction, alongside narrowing sensitivity to unused contrasts."],"development1-15":["An **emergentist** account explains organized behavior through learning mechanisms sensitive to regularities in experience. An association strengthens when events reliably occur together.","Associative learning mechanisms are sensitive to statistical regularities in the environment. A regularity is a pattern in what occurs together or predicts another event.","In the neuron illustration, food already activates a salivation response. Repeated pairing allows the bell-related connection to become stronger, so the bell can help activate that response.","In the conditioning illustration, the food-related pathway already supports salivation. Pairing bell-related activity with that response strengthens the relevant connection until the bell can contribute more effectively.","Speech learning requires extending this principle to pattern recognition. The relevant input is not merely a reward but the distribution and co-occurrence of acoustic properties across many sounds.","The learning problem is how such changes can support pattern recognition. Speech categories require organization across many variable examples, not only one stimulus being paired with one response."],"development1-16":["The vowel-space plot places productions using F1 and F2. A vowel category forms a cloud because no speaker produces exactly the same resonance pattern every time.","A vowel is a distribution of productions in a space described by the first and second formants. Its members are similar without having one identical acoustic measurement.","A statistical learner can track where observations cluster. A **prototype**, a particularly representative example, could correspond to the distribution's mean or most frequent region.","A prototypical /a/ can be understood as the mean or modal region of the distribution. Mean is the average; mode is the most frequent region.","This turns category learning into discovering structure in variable input. Overlapping clouds explain why a single formant pair may remain ambiguous even after useful categories have formed.","Learning the statistics means becoming sensitive to that clustering through exposure. The category's useful center can emerge from the input rather than being a single token supplied in advance."],"development1-17":["Infants passively heard a continuum distributed around either one peak or two. **Unimodal** exposure emphasizes one central cluster; **bimodal** exposure emphasizes two clusters.","Bimodal input provides evidence for two categories, while unimodal input provides evidence for one. The manipulation changes the relative concentration of exposure across the continuum.","Test trials alternated tokens 3 and 6 or repeated a token. Those critical tokens occurred equally often during exposure, so their individual familiarity does not explain the group contrast.","Tokens 3 and 6 are equally frequent in both familiarization conditions. The test alternates those tokens or repeats one, so the comparison isolates how the surrounding distribution changes their relationship.","Only the bimodal group showed clear sensitivity to the alternating versus nonalternating test. The distribution changed whether the same two sounds were treated as distinct, supporting learning from statistical structure.","Only bimodally exposed infants show clear sensitivity to the test distinction. Unimodal exposure leads to behavior consistent with treating the critical sounds as members of one category."],"development1-18":["Infants exposed to live, interactive Mandarin showed phonetic learning more clearly than groups receiving television or audio exposure. The contrast concerns the context of language experience, not simply the presence of sound.","Live Mandarin sessions combine the speech sounds with an interactive speaker. Auditory and audiovisual recordings supply language input without the same reciprocal exchange.","In another study, mothers' responses either followed their infant's vocalizations or occurred on a noncontingent schedule. **Contingent** feedback depends on the infant's own action; it increased the quantity and complexity of vocalizations.","In the maternal-feedback comparison, contingent responses follow the infant's own vocalizations. A yoked or noncontingent response schedule does not preserve that relationship between the infant's action and the social consequence.","Social exchange can help by reinforcing behavior or directing attention to useful information. These mechanisms connect other people to the learning process rather than treating exposure as an undifferentiated count of sounds.","The explanations are that social contact can be innately reinforcing or that humans are especially effective at using social cues to learn. Both proposals make the interaction part of the learning mechanism, rather than simply another measure of exposure duration."],"development1-19":["A learner must discover which properties distinguish categories. Speaker identity and speech rate vary widely but do not define the same linguistic contrasts as phonetic cues.","What stimulus properties can be contrastive is the first constraint. The learner has to attend to category-relevant patterns without building a new phoneme category for every talker or speaking rate.","Earlier learning changes later learning by making some distinctions easier to preserve or acquire. This creates a connection between statistical learning and sensitive periods.","Constraints based on earlier learning mean that the current organization changes what is easy to learn next. This connects the development of categories with later sensitive-period questions.","Social and interactive context also changes what is learned. A constrained statistical account therefore includes the properties attended to, the learner's existing organization, and the context in which the input arrives.","Social and interactive contexts provide another constraint. The same acoustic information can have different learning consequences depending on how it enters an attentive, reciprocal exchange."],"development2-01":["Language development includes separating words from speech, linking them to meanings, and combining them into structures. Success at one problem does not automatically solve the next.","Words are recurring sound forms, morphemes are meaningful grammatical pieces, and constructions organize combinations of forms. Learning their relationships requires more than storing isolated sound–object pairs.","**Morphemes** are meaningful grammatical pieces, and **constructions** are patterns that connect forms with uses or meanings. The experiments ask what children know before their own utterances become long and fluent.","A child has to discover where a unit begins, what it refers to, and how it can combine with other units. The experiments separate these tasks so that success on one is not mistaken for success on all.","The developmental evidence also separates what a child understands from what the child can say. A short produced utterance may coexist with knowledge that supports a more complex interpretation."],"development2-02":["The acoustic signal has no consistent physical boundary between every pair of words. Closures and pauses can occur within words as well as between them.","There are no physical boundaries between words in the ordinary speech stream. A temporary closure can be caused by a consonant inside a word, rather than a pause separating complete words.","A learner therefore cannot simply split the waveform wherever silence appears. **Segmentation** means discovering word-sized units within this connected stream.","An experienced listener recognizes many boundaries without noticing this problem. A beginner must discover recurring units before having the vocabulary that makes segmentation feel effortless.","The problem is harder for a beginner who does not already know the words. Regularities within the signal must help establish the units that will later support recognition.","The learning question is therefore how regularities in connected speech can identify useful groupings. Pauses, stress, phonotactics, and syllable predictability supply different kinds of evidence rather than interchangeable printed spaces."],"development2-03":["**Phonotactics** concerns which sound sequences occur within a language's words. An unusual sequence can suggest that a word boundary lies between its sounds.","Words sometimes appear in isolation, but that does not provide isolated examples of every word. Phonotactics adds information because some sound sequences are much less likely inside words than across boundaries.","English often stresses the first syllable of a word. Infants familiarized with kingdom recognized it in connected passages more clearly than infants familiarized with device, whose stress falls later.","In the kingdom passage, the familiarized word begins with the stressed syllable. Device has a different stress pattern, and infants did not show the same recognition preference for its passage.","Stress can therefore help locate word beginnings in English. Isolated words and phonotactic regularities supply additional cues, and different languages can assign these cues different usefulness.","The result supports using stress as a word-beginning cue in English. It is a useful distributional tendency rather than a claim that every English word or every language puts stress in the same position."],"development2-04":["A **transitional probability** is how likely one syllable is to follow another. In pretty baby, the first syllable of pretty strongly predicts its own second syllable.","The probability of syllable B following syllable A can be estimated by dividing the frequency of AB by the frequency of A. The denominator asks how many opportunities A supplied for that particular continuation.","The final syllable of pretty can instead be followed by many words, making one specific across-word continuation less reliable. The frequency ratios compare a pair's occurrences with the occurrences of its starting syllable.","In pretty baby, the second syllable of pretty is strongly predicted by its first. The final syllable of pretty has many possible continuations across word boundaries, so baby is less uniquely predicted.","High within-word predictability and lower across-word predictability can help locate boundaries. The learner tracks conditional relationships rather than simply counting which syllables are common.","A boundary can therefore coincide with a drop in predictability. This method uses relationships between observations rather than simply treating the most frequent syllable as a word."],"development2-05":["A syllable is often more predictable after another syllable inside a word than after a word ending. This makes a drop in predictability a useful segmentation cue.","The likelihood of a following syllable tends to be greater within than between words. A learned sequence has relatively reliable internal continuations, while its final syllable can be followed by several different words.","The road ahead versus a head and ice cream versus I scream show that acoustic sequences can support multiple divisions. Context can change which division is meaningful.","Road ahead and road a head illustrate how the same sequence can be divided differently. Ice cream and I scream make the same point with another familiar ambiguity.","Statistical segmentation is therefore probabilistic. Several sources of information can support an interpretation, and the same sound sequence does not have to carry one unavoidable boundary structure.","These examples do not remove the statistical tendency. They show why the cue must be combined with other information instead of functioning as a boundary marker that is always correct."],"development2-06":["Infants heard continuously presented artificial speech made from simple consonant–vowel syllables. The stream was monotone, without boundary-defining changes in pitch, amplitude, duration, or pauses.","The stream was monotone: pitch, duration, and amplitude did not mark the trained boundaries. Simple consonant–vowel syllables also reduced phonotactic clues, and continuous presentation removed pauses.","Repeated three-syllable units such as tokibu and gopila created strong internal relationships. Boundaries were available through the transition statistics rather than ordinary speech cues.","The experimenter constructed three-syllable words such as tokibu, gikoba, and gopila. Their recurring internal syllable relationships remain in the signal even after the other usual cues are controlled.","This design tests whether statistical structure is sufficient to affect segmentation. A successful response cannot be attributed to a pause conveniently marking each artificial word.","This is what isolating statistical information means. If infants later distinguish the units, the explanation has to consider the regularities that the artificial stream actually supplies."],"development2-07":["The printed syllable stream illustrates what the infant hears without inserted word spaces. The same small set of three-syllable units recurs in different orders.","Tokibugikobagopila looks like an uninterrupted string because no spaces identify the intended units. The auditory presentation likewise does not tell the infant explicitly where each word starts.","A starting syllable reliably predicts the rest of its unit, while a unit ending may lead to several possible next units. These conditional patterns are present even when the stream looks like one continuous string.","Within a repeated unit, one syllable strongly predicts the next. When the unit ends, its last syllable can be followed by the beginnings of several different units.","The learner's problem is to discover recurring groupings from those relationships. The experimenter knows the units, but the infant must recover them from exposure.","Exposure therefore contains a structure to discover. The learner can use repeated conditional patterns without first being handed a list of named words."],"development2-08":["Coloring the recurring units reveals the structure already present in the unsegmented stream. The colors are an explanatory aid; they were not an extra cue in the infant's auditory input.","The colored version separates the same string into recurring units. The colors identify the experimenter's intended groupings without changing the statistics of the underlying syllable sequence.","Across repetitions, syllables inside a unit stay together more reliably than syllables straddling a boundary. The same sequence therefore supports different predictions at internal and boundary positions.","Those visual groupings help explain the input, but the infant's task is auditory. The actual evidence for a unit comes from reliable internal transitions and less reliable transitions across its boundary.","The comparison between the plain and colored versions separates what the input contains statistically from what it explicitly marks. Repetition can reveal structure without audible spaces.","Comparing the unmarked and marked versions makes the learning problem concrete: discover the organization visible to the experimenter from the regularities audible to the participant."],"development2-09":["Testing **transitional probabilities** requires separating the predictability of a syllable sequence from how often it was heard. Hearing a familiar sequence more often could produce a preference without the infant having used its internal transitions.","Half of the artificial words are repeated more frequently than the other half. The test compares words with **part-words matched for frequency of occurrence**. A part-word spans a boundary instead of corresponding to one of the intended words.","The stream includes tokibu, gikoba, and gopila. **GOPILA** appears twice, and the boundary-spanning sequence **BU-GIKO** also appears twice. Those equal occurrence counts make simple frequency an insufficient explanation of their difference.","Within GOPILA, the relevant transitions both have probability **2/2**. Each observed GO is followed by PI, and each observed PI is followed by LA. For the cross-boundary transition, GI follows BU on only **2/4** occasions, because BU also leads into another following word.","Transitional probability asks how often the next syllable follows this particular preceding syllable. The word has more reliable internal transitions than the equally frequent part-word. Distinguishing them therefore provides evidence about sensitivity to that predictability, which can help locate word boundaries."],"development2-10":["Infants looked longer toward part-word test items than toward trained words. Here the longer response is a **novelty preference**, showing that the groups of syllables were treated differently.","A novelty effect means longer looking toward something less familiar in the relevant respect. Here the unfamiliar property belongs to the boundary-spanning structure of the part-word.","Because part-words were matched in occurrence frequency, simple repetition count does not explain why they were less familiar in structure. The conditional syllable relationships distinguish them.","Both test strings have been heard with matched frequencies, so simple exposure to the whole string does not explain why they are treated differently. Their internal transitional probabilities remain different.","The result supports statistical segmentation. Longer looking does not always mean greater familiarity; its interpretation depends on the task and exposure design.","The result supports learning the statistical regularities of the speech stream. The response is meaningful because the exposure and control conditions specify what could have made one item novel."],"development2-11":["Finding a stable spoken form such as cat identifies a recurring unit. It does not determine which object, property, or event that form refers to.","A sound sequence can be segmented correctly before its meaning is known. Recognizing a recurring cat form does not yet establish which part of the scene it names.","**Reference** is the relation between an expression and what the speaker intends it to pick out. Word learning requires connecting the discovered form to an appropriate interpretation.","The learner now faces a mapping problem between a linguistic unit and a referent. A referent is what an expression picks out in a particular communicative situation.","The next experiments therefore move from recognizing repeated sound patterns to using the social and conceptual context in which words are spoken.","Segmentation supplies a candidate form; reference learning supplies an interpretation. Keeping the tasks separate explains why finding a word in the sea of sounds is only one step in acquisition."],"development2-12":["The **inductive problem of early word learning** is that a scene is compatible with many possible meanings. Hearing a sound while looking at an animal does not specify which aspect of the scene the speaker intends to name.","The animal could be called **CAT**, **PET**, **ANIMAL**, or **FELIX**. These choices refer to different levels: a kind of animal, its relationship to people, a broad category, or one particular individual.","Other possibilities include **FURRY**, **WHISKERS**, **LEG**, **WALKING**, and **ALIVE**. A new word might refer to a property, a body part, an action, or a state rather than the whole animal.","Quine’s **gavagai problem** expresses this uncertainty about reference. An unfamiliar word and a visible scene alone leave several interpretations open. The **hypothesis space** is the set of meanings the learner could consider.","Word learning therefore needs ways of narrowing that space. Attention, the speaker’s intention, and assumptions about what new labels usually name can help select a useful interpretation. The following word-learning strategies address how the child gets from many compatible possibilities to a more specific meaning."],"development2-13":["**Joint attention** means sharing an attentional focus with another person. Gaze following begins around the age indicated in the lecture and helps a child connect communication with an attended object.","Sharing an attentional focus with another person makes the scene communicative. The child can observe that the adult is directing attention toward something, not merely producing sounds nearby.","Looking where a speaker looks is useful because a word is intentionally directed toward something. The relevant scene includes the speaker's relation to the object, not only the object's presence.","Gaze following helps identify that focus. It provides a link between the speaker's expression and the entity the speaker appears to intend.","Shared attention therefore supplies evidence about reference. It helps distinguish what the adult means from other things happening at the same moment.","This signals an understanding of reference because the word is treated as being about an object for another person. That is more informative than simply associating the label with whichever object happens to be most salient to the infant."],"development2-14":["In the joint-attention condition, an adult interacting with the infant labeled the object the infant was handling. In the other condition, a voice behind a screen supplied the label.","In the joint-attention group, an adult interacts with the baby and labels the object the baby is playing with. In the no-joint-attention group, a voice behind a screen provides the label.","Eighteen-month-olds learned the new word in the joint-attention condition but not in the no-joint-attention condition. Simply hearing a label while touching an object was not equally effective.","Both situations pair the word with the object in the infant's immediate experience. They differ in whether a visible, engaged person establishes a shared naming interaction.","The result connects learning to a communicative interaction. Shared attention helps make the spoken form an intentional reference to the object.","Learning in the interactive group shows why joint attention optimizes word learning. The naming context helps make the object–word relationship relevant as an intentional communicative link."],"development2-15":["In **follow-in labeling**, the speaker looks at and names the toy already occupying the infant's attention. In **discrepant labeling**, the speaker attends to a different toy, such as one in a bucket.","Follow-in labeling means that the adult looks at the toy already occupying the infant's attention. Discrepant labeling means that the adult looks elsewhere, such as toward a toy in a bucket.","The contrast separates the infant's current focus from the speaker's intended referent. A purely simultaneous-association strategy would favor the infant's own toy even when the adult names something else.","The two conditions separate the child's current focus from the speaker's intended focus. A learner relying only on personal attention would not use the discrepancy to redirect the label.","Following the speaker's gaze provides another route to the mapping. The key question is whether infants use information about another person's intention rather than only their own momentary attention.","Speaker gaze is therefore evidence about intention. The experiment asks whether infants use that evidence to narrow the hypothesis space for a new word."],"development2-16":["A gaze-direction demonstration asks which object the speaker is looking toward when giving a new label. Eye direction can distinguish an intended referent from a competing object.","Eye direction can identify an object other than the one the infant is handling. The adult's visual focus supplies information about what the new label is intended to name.","The informative comparison is between the child's focus and the adult's focus. They can coincide, or the child can use the adult's gaze to redirect the interpretation.","The inference is that a speaker's word can concern the speaker's target, even when another object dominates the child's own immediate activity. This prevents simple simultaneity from being the whole explanation.","This page marks a demonstration in the source PDF. The underlying concept is intentional reference through gaze; a still PDF does not reproduce the original moving demonstration.","The gaze-direction demonstration belongs to the same contrast as follow-in and discrepant labeling. It illustrates how observing another person's attention can change the selected referent."],"development2-17":["A new label can occur while an adult searches among objects or reacts differently to them. Facial expression and behavior help identify what the adult intends.","Affective cues are signs of emotion, while behavioral cues include searching, handling, and reactions to objects. Both can communicate which object an adult was trying to identify.","Infants use those cues to select a referent for the novel word. The relevant information is not only which object was physically present when the sound occurred.","Infants can use an adult's facial expression to distinguish an intended referent from other objects encountered during the interaction. Not every visible object is an equally good label candidate.","Learning therefore includes interpreting an interaction. An adult's satisfaction, attention, or goal can narrow the candidate meanings of a new expression.","The learner is therefore interpreting the adult's goal. Information about satisfaction or expectation helps determine what the word is about, rather than merely adding another simultaneous sensory event."],"development2-18":["An intentional-cues demonstration highlights that naming occurs within goal-directed behavior. The learner observes what the speaker is trying to find or communicate.","An intentional-cues demonstration presents naming within an unfolding activity. The meaning of a reaction depends on what the adult was trying to do and which object completes that goal.","A response that identifies the intended object follows the adult's goal rather than treating every encounter as an equally good label–object pairing. This connects behavior and affect to reference.","A child can connect a new expression with the successful target rather than with every object the adult looked at. That requires tracking the interaction instead of matching a label to an arbitrary recent sight.","The PDF provides the demonstration title rather than its original video. The teaching point is the use of the speaker's intention as evidence about a word's meaning.","This is another way to narrow reference. Speaker intention can be expressed through action and affect as well as through gaze direction alone."],"development2-19":["The **whole-object assumption** is a default interpretation that a new noun names an entire object rather than one of its parts, properties, or actions.","Word-learning strategies are default ways of narrowing possible meanings. They reduce the number of hypotheses that must be considered when a speaker introduces a label.","It narrows the mapping problem immediately. A novel word spoken near an unfamiliar toy is initially linked to the toy as a whole instead of every visible feature separately.","The whole-object assumption favors a new noun as the name of an entire entity. It does not initially select a detachable part, a texture, or a movement as the intended meaning.","A default is a starting preference, not an unchangeable rule. More informative contextual or linguistic evidence can support a different meaning.","This makes the otherwise broad mapping problem tractable. Later evidence can refine the interpretation, so a default starting point does not prevent a learner from acquiring words for parts and properties."],"development2-20":["**Mutual exclusivity** is a default tendency to map a new word onto an object without an already known name. If one object is familiar and another unnamed, the unfamiliar one is a plausible referent.","As a default, objects can have only one name in the mutual-exclusivity strategy. The implication is that new words map to nameless objects when a known alternative is available.","The strategy reduces competition among possible mappings. Existing vocabulary becomes evidence about where a new label is likely to belong.","If dog is already a name the child knows, a different novel label favors an unfamiliar object in the display. Existing vocabulary changes the relative plausibility of the candidate referents.","The lecture places its emergence in the later portion of the second year. The essential idea is the inference from known naming relations, rather than a requirement that an object literally can never have more than one label.","The strategy emerges during the later part of the second year in the account presented. Its important function is using prior naming knowledge to resolve ambiguity, rather than imposing an exceptionless rule on all adult vocabulary."],"development2-21":["Suppose a known object and an unfamiliar object are available when a novel label is requested. The strategy favors the object without a known label.","A familiar and an unfamiliar object create an informative choice. A request using a novel label leaves the familiar object's known name as evidence against choosing it again by default.","This follows from treating the familiar object's existing name as a reason to seek another referent. The choice therefore uses vocabulary knowledge to solve a new mapping problem.","The child can favor the unnamed object without having received an explicit definition of the new word. The inference uses a relationship between the alternatives.","The source page introduces a demonstration. Its concept can be understood through the decision structure even though the original video is not embedded in the uploaded PDF.","This demonstration therefore connects a word-learning bias to observable behavior. The relevant outcome is where the child maps the new label when the candidate objects differ in existing naming status."],"development2-22":["Menn distinguishes a collection of relevant words, memorized formulas, productive combinations, and stable constructions. The same short utterance can fit different explanations depending on its context.","Just a bunch of relevant words, formulas, word combinations, and stable constructions are different descriptions of early production. Similar-looking short utterances can reflect different kinds of knowledge.","Up, pick you up, and mommy up differ in length but do not by themselves reveal the child's full system. A formula can be reproduced without flexible control of all its parts.","Pick you up can be a familiar formula, while other combinations may reveal more flexible organization. The surrounding activity helps determine what the child meant and whether components were used productively.","Context and generalization help distinguish these possibilities. The goal is to determine whether a child can use a pattern productively beyond one familiar routine.","A full description of context is necessary because word count alone does not identify the underlying construction. Generalization to new materials gives additional evidence about how flexible the pattern is."],"development2-23":["Children producing only one word at a time can already use basic transitive word order in comprehension. Their spoken output is therefore an incomplete measure of their structural knowledge.","One-word production and transitive comprehension are not the same task. A child may fail to produce a long sentence while still using its order to interpret an action.","In a sentence–scene comparison, word order can specify which participant acts and which receives the action. A child does not have to produce the full sentence to show that distinction.","A transitive frame links an agent to an action and an affected participant. Correct comprehension involves mapping those roles onto the observed scene.","This separates linguistic understanding from production demands. Short utterances do not automatically imply that the child has no representation of relations involving several words.","This can exceed what the child's current production makes obvious. The result therefore supports testing knowledge through more than the length of the child's spoken output."],"development2-24":["Two-year-olds heard a sentence such as the duck is gorping the bunny while viewing novel actions with reversed actor–recipient roles. **Gorping** is unfamiliar, so its meaning cannot simply be recalled from a known phrase.","The duck is gorping the bunny uses a new verb with a transitive word-order frame. The pictured events reverse which animal acts on which, so the nouns alone do not choose the answer.","Children looked longer at the action in which the duck acted on the bunny. The transitive word-order pattern identified the roles even with a new verb.","Looking longer at the duck-on-bunny event shows the use of sentence structure to assign roles. The unfamiliar verb reduces the possibility of recognizing one memorized verb-specific phrase.","This is evidence of generalization beyond memorized utterances. Their MLU (mean length of utterance) is around two. Understanding the relation can exceed a short production such as duck gorp bunny.","A mean length of utterance around two describes the child's typical production, not a strict upper limit on comprehension. The generalization test asks whether the structural pattern can be applied beyond a familiar utterance."],"l1-critical-01":["An **L1** is a first language. Studies of delayed access to any language ask whether the timing of early experience changes later attainment.","A first-language timing question differs from learning another language after a well-established L1. The initial system has to be organized through accessible linguistic experience.","The lecture first uses visual development to explain experience-dependent organization, then applies the timing question to brain injury and delayed language exposure.","The lecture compares developmental windows, brain reorganization, and unusually late input histories. Each provides evidence about how experience and developmental state interact.","The visual example supplies a concrete mechanism for thinking about early effects. The language cases then ask which aspects of that timing logic carry over to acquisition and recovery."],"l1-critical-02":["A strict **critical period** has a discrete onset and offset, with relevant learning confined to that interval. Outside it, the proposed system is insensitive to that learning experience.","A critical period has a discrete, programmed onset and offset. Sensitivity to the relevant environmental influence is confined to that interval in the strict version of the claim.","A **sensitive** or **optimal period** instead makes some ages especially favorable while allowing learning at other ages. Its timing and effectiveness may also depend on experience.","A sensitive or optimal period permits learning throughout life but makes some windows particularly effective. Its onset and offset can be gradual and can themselves be affected by experience.","Evidence of an age advantage does not by itself choose between these descriptions. Continued improvement after the favored window fits a sensitive-period account more naturally than an absolute cutoff.","The distinction turns on what later learning is possible, not simply whether children outperform adults. An early advantage with measurable later change supports a different claim from an absolute closing of sensitivity."],"l1-critical-03":["**Amblyopia** is poor vision in an eye despite normal peripheral function at the time it is assessed. Early abnormal input can leave a lasting cortical consequence.","Strabismus, cataract, and anisometropia can disrupt the normal input relationship between the eyes. They differ in their peripheral cause but can each change early visual experience.","Strabismus misaligns the eyes, a cataract clouds input, and anisometropia gives the eyes different optical properties. Each can disrupt the normal relationship between the inputs during development.","Amblyopia describes the lasting poor visual function rather than only the original optical or alignment problem. The brain developed while one input was disadvantaged.","Repairing the initial condition does not necessarily restore ordinary vision. The lasting impairment reflects how the brain organized itself while the input was abnormal.","This separates the initial disturbance from its sequela, or later consequence. A temporary interruption can leave a persistent functional problem after the initiating condition has been treated."],"l1-critical-04":["Inputs from the eyes compete for cortical representation. If one eye supplies abnormal input, the other can gain an increasing representational advantage.","The competing inputs do not develop independently. Abnormal input from one eye changes the balance of cortical responses in favor of the other eye.","Restoring the disadvantaged eye's physical function does not immediately reverse that organization. The stronger input continues to dominate the existing competitive system.","Colonization describes that representational shift. When the disadvantaged input becomes normal again, it enters a system whose organization has already been changed by the earlier imbalance.","The sequence links a temporary early disturbance to a persistent later outcome. What matters is not only what information arrives now but the representation established by earlier experience.","Normal current experience is therefore not equivalent to a normal developmental history. The cortical competition provides a mechanism linking an early input difference with a later persistent disadvantage."],"l1-critical-05":["Patching the better eye reduces its competitive advantage. The weaker eye then has more opportunity to drive processing and benefit from the brain's plasticity.","Intermittently patching the good eye reduces its access to the competitive system. The affected eye is no longer continually opposed by the strongest alternative input.","The treatment is generally more effective early in development. Later in childhood the already-established imbalance can be harder to alter through this intervention.","This permits the brain's own plasticity to alter the balance. The treatment changes experience at the system level, rather than directly correcting the lens or alignment that originally caused the problem.","The mechanism is experience-dependent rebalancing. Covering the stronger eye does not repair the weaker eye's optics directly; it changes how the cortical system uses the competing inputs.","Greater effectiveness early in development identifies an optimal window for this intervention. The next evidence asks whether other kinds of experience can still produce improvement after that window."],"l1-critical-06":["Levi and Polat examined intensive practice in adults with amblyopia. Repeated psychophysical training in the affected eye improved task performance and aspects of visual function.","Long-term, intensive training uses repeated psychophysical judgments with the affected eye. A psychophysical task relates a precisely specified stimulus to a behavioral response.","The graph relates performance to extended training, showing that substantial exposure can change an adult system that had developed abnormally. The relevant comparison is improvement with practice.","Performance changes over many trials, and some benefits extend to more general visual function. The measured improvement shows that the adult system can still be modified by suitable experience.","Adult plasticity is therefore reduced or differently accessed, rather than necessarily absent. A sensitive-period description accommodates both an early advantage and later learning under suitable conditions.","Plasticity is not abolished entirely even well into adulthood. The timing account therefore has to explain both the early treatment advantage and the later conditions under which change can still occur."],"l1-critical-07":["The cited work examines video-game training in adult amblyopia. Game performance engages repeated visual discrimination and attention in the affected system.","The game-training example engages visual decisions repeatedly over extended practice. It provides a different route to experience-dependent improvement from the narrowly repeated laboratory judgments.","Its place in the argument is adult experience producing visual change. The example extends the idea of training beyond one narrowly repeated laboratory judgment.","The cited outcome concerns visual acuity and visual attention in adult amblyopia. Those are behavioral functions that can be measured before and after training.","The conceptual connection is to plasticity after the optimal developmental period. Improvement later in life distinguishes an especially effective early window from a permanent end to all learning.","Its relevance is to continued learning, not to a claim that every form of visual input is equally effective. The type and organization of later experience are part of what makes adult plasticity observable."],"l1-critical-08":["An early disturbance can change the experience-dependent organization of the visual system. Persistent poor function is a consequence of that developmental history.","The long-term loss of function is a consequence of a temporary deficit during experience-dependent organization. This is why correcting the current peripheral problem may leave the functional difficulty behind.","Treatment works most readily within an earlier window, but appropriately structured adult training can also improve function. The timing and the nature of experience both matter.","Treatment is most effective during a limited developmental window, linking efficiency of reorganization to age. Later specialized experience can nevertheless reveal remaining capacity for improvement.","Amblyopia therefore illustrates a **sensitive period** with competitive dynamics. It supplies a concrete mechanism to consider when interpreting age effects in other developing systems.","A sensitive-period account captures both findings. It describes an optimal time without requiring the brain to become completely unchangeable afterward."],"l1-critical-09":["The language question is whether experience at particular ages is especially important for building an effective language system. Lenneberg connected this possibility to developmental neurology.","Lenneberg attempted to relate a language acquisition device to developmental neurology. The question is whether the timing of neural organization affects when language can be acquired most effectively.","Deprivation cases ask what happens when accessible language is absent early. Delayed sign-language access provides a different kind of evidence from histories of severe neglect.","Early deprivation provides a second kind of evidence. Neglected children and deaf children without accessible sign input can begin substantial language experience unusually late.","These comparisons concern access to a usable language, not simply whether someone has heard speech. A child needs input the developing system can actually perceive and learn from.","These histories concern access to a usable language, not only the presence of audible speech. The input must be available to the learner's perceptual and communicative system."],"l1-critical-10":["Lenneberg linked stage-like language milestones with the changing brain. An orderly developmental sequence made a maturational contribution plausible.","Biological Foundations of Language connects stage-like patterns of language development with what was then known about the developing brain. Ordered milestones made a maturational contribution plausible.","A **maturational** account attributes change partly to the organism's biological development. The proposal was that the ability to acquire language might open and close in relation to that development.","A biological program is a proposed organizing influence on developmental change. It does not mean that every later utterance is encoded as a fixed sentence before experience occurs.","The evidence combined linguistic milestones and neurological observations. The important causal question is which changes enable early learning and which reduce later reorganization.","The question is what maturation permits and constrains. Acquisition still concerns how experience produces the grammar and vocabulary of a particular language within that developing system."],"l1-critical-11":["Early brain-development data largely came from postmortem tissue. Measures such as neuronal size and packing density changed rapidly during early childhood.","Typical brain-development observations came largely from postmortem tissue. Measures such as neuron size and packing density described broad differences across the available ages.","Lenneberg considered these changes potentially permissive for the beginning of language acquisition. A permissive change enables a process; it does not have to specify every word or rule that will be learned.","Rapid changes before age two were interpreted as potentially permissive for language acquisition. Permissive means helping make the process possible, rather than supplying a specific learned word.","The plotted age coverage is sparse, including a large gap between childhood and adulthood. The proposal links biological change and learning capacity without a continuously sampled developmental record.","The missing observations between early childhood and adolescence limit the precision of the timeline. The proposal is a synthesis of the available evidence, not a continuously observed neural opening and closing event."],"l1-critical-12":["**Hemispherectomy** is removal of a cerebral hemisphere, discussed here in the treatment of severe seizures. The language outcome depends strongly on when the damage or surgery occurs.","Children can develop substantial language after very early damage, including loss of left-hemisphere tissue normally associated with language. The developing system can assign important functions to remaining tissue.","Adults show a very different pattern after comparable loss. The contrast suggests that the same anatomical damage has different consequences depending on the system’s developmental state.","Lenneberg related this change to **cerebral dominance**, the developing asymmetry of language organization between the hemispheres. Earlier organization was proposed to allow more flexibility before adult-like specialization became established.","The causal idea is functional reassignment: a less fixed early organization provides more opportunity for other tissue to support language. The developmental contrast therefore contributes to the proposal that biological maturation changes the conditions under which language can be acquired or reorganized."],"l1-critical-13":["A **perinatal stroke** occurs around birth. Children with such injuries often have better language outcomes than adults with comparable damage.","Perinatal includes the prenatal period through the first weeks after birth in this discussion. Both left- and right-hemisphere injuries can disrupt early language development.","Better does not necessarily mean fully typical. Studies show early deficits after injury to either hemisphere and later outcomes that may remain in a low-normal range.","Some outcomes remain in the low-normal range rather than following a fully typical trajectory. A child can improve substantially and still show a persistent developmental cost.","Early plasticity can support substantial function while leaving persistent costs. The comparison distinguishes relative resilience from complete compensation.","Early resilience is therefore a relative advantage over adult injury, not a guarantee of complete catching up. The level and kind of remaining difficulty matter when describing plasticity."],"l1-critical-14":["Genie experienced severe deprivation and abuse with extremely restricted early linguistic interaction. The case offered an opportunity to observe language learning after unusually delayed access.","The early deprivation was unusually severe, involving restricted social and linguistic experience as well as abuse. Genie did not simply begin an ordinary language class later than other children.","Her later learning involved vocabulary, comprehension, and production developing at different rates. These components must be considered separately rather than summarized as language present or absent.","Later observations tracked separate parts of language learning. Words, grammatical relations, and spoken production could improve at different rates.","The central timing question is whether the delayed first-language history especially affects grammar. The severe deprivation history is also part of what makes this case different from ordinary late instruction.","This makes the case relevant to the timing of first-language organization. Explaining the pattern requires following what was learned, not treating her later language as wholly present or wholly absent."],"l1-critical-15":["Genie developed comprehension of many words, some prepositions, and aspects of morphology. Plurals were a relative area of success in the observations described.","Hundreds of understood words show a substantial ability to establish later form–meaning relationships. Understanding a word can be demonstrated without having to produce a long sentence.","**Morphology** concerns meaningful word parts, such as plural marking. Understanding a plural distinction is more than recognizing an isolated object name.","Plural comprehension shows sensitivity to one aspect of morphology, and prepositions relate entities in ways beyond isolated object naming. These are specific kinds of knowledge acquired during observation.","The case therefore shows substantial later learning. The pattern is uneven: acquiring vocabulary and selected grammatical relations does not imply that the full system develops at the same rate.","The pattern is therefore not a total failure of learning after childhood. It sets up the important comparison with the much slower growth of broader grammatical organization."],"l1-critical-16":["Genie's production remained far behind comprehension. Some comprehension advantage occurs in typical development, but her early punishment for making sounds also bears directly on speaking.","Production lagged severely behind comprehension. Speaking requires organizing and executing an utterance, while an understanding task can reveal a relation without requiring that entire output.","Grammatical abilities grew much more slowly than vocabulary. The contrast is between components of acquisition rather than a total inability to learn anything after childhood.","Being punished for making sounds is also directly relevant to the developmental history of speech. It makes the speaking trajectory different from a simple test of whether a grammar rule is known.","This difference made the case influential in discussion of L1 timing. A later learner could acquire many meanings while still struggling with the productive organization of sentences.","Grammatical development was much slower than vocabulary development. That component difference is the central observation motivating a timing claim about first-language grammar."],"l1-critical-17":["The grammar–vocabulary disparity is consistent with the idea that early experience is especially important for first-language grammar. It is the pattern of learning, not simply the age at observation, that motivates this interpretation.","The observed disparity is widely interpreted as suggestive of specific difficulty acquiring grammar after an early window. Vocabulary success and grammatical difficulty occurred within the same later learning history.","Genie also had a history of severe abuse, disrupted social development, and uncertain prior capacities. Those factors affect both the input and the learning situation.","Other aspects of the history include neglect, abuse, and questions about prior learning capacities. These affect how confidently one cause can be assigned to the pattern.","The observation period ended when funding stopped. The case provides evidence about observed development over that interval, while other delayed-language populations help test which aspects of the pattern generalize.","The study ended after four years of observation when funding was withdrawn. The positive finding is therefore the development actually measured over that interval, with other populations needed to test its wider generality."],"l1-critical-18":["Some deaf children encounter an accessible first language only when sign language becomes available later. A comparison group learned spoken language earlier and later acquired sign after becoming deaf.","The late first-language group had not acquired an accessible early language. The late second-language comparison had an early spoken language before later becoming deaf and learning sign.","The groups may begin sign at similar ages but differ in prior language experience. Late first-language learners have greater difficulty than learners who already established another language.","Starting ASL at a similar later age does not equate those earlier histories. One group is building an initial linguistic foundation; the other is learning an additional system after such a foundation already exists.","This separates age of learning a particular language from age of access to any language. Early linguistic experience provides a foundation whose effects can persist into later learning and adulthood.","Better performance after an early L1 supports the importance of early accessible linguistic experience. The difference is not explained simply by saying that everyone started the particular sign language late."],"l1-critical-19":["Brain-injury recovery differs greatly between childhood and adulthood. Delayed first-language access is also associated with persistent difficulties, especially in complex language learning.","Recovery after early brain injury differs from recovery in adulthood, and lack of input does not appear to keep the first-language window open indefinitely. Development continues while the needed experience is missing.","Simply withholding input does not appear to preserve an indefinitely open first-language window. Time passes and the system develops even when normal experience is missing.","The cases address different operations and histories, so they should be related through a specified learning question. Vocabulary growth, grammatical organization, and neural reassignment are not interchangeable measures.","These findings support an important role for early accessible language. The sensitive-period distinction remains useful because later learning can occur without reproducing the outcome of early acquisition.","The converging point is the importance of early access to language. Sensitive-period terminology leaves room for substantial later learning without equating it with the outcome of typical early acquisition."],"l1-critical-20":["Second-language learning lets the timing question be examined in many more people. Learners already possess a language but differ in when and how they encounter another.","Second-language learners already have a linguistic system before the new language is introduced. This gives the timing question a different starting condition from delayed L1 acquisition.","The next comparison therefore asks whether early L1 experience preserves later capacity, or whether maturation changes learning even after a first language is established.","Their early L1 experience can be used to test an exercise account, in which early use preserves later learning capacity. A maturational account instead allows later capacity to change despite early use.","The next material therefore asks whether age effects reflect only a lack of early practice or a developmental change that can also affect people who acquired an L1 normally."],"l1-critical-21":["The source lists films and further material related to Genie, unusual language histories, and plasticity. These extend the theme of what early experience changes.","The listed materials extend the examples of unusual input histories and later plasticity. Genie and other deprivation cases concern how access to language interacts with social development.","The common question remains whether later experience can restore a function, build a new one, or reveal residual learning. These are different outcomes and should be distinguished when connecting a case to a sensitive-period account.","The distinction between recovering a former ability and building a first system remains important. A benefit from later experience can reflect different forms of learning depending on what preceded it.","The relevant theme is experience-dependent development. A later behavioral outcome is interpreted through the early history, the intervening experience, and the function tested now."],"sentence-01":["**Sentence processing** assigns structure and meaning as words arrive. A listener or reader often has to choose among several possible relationships before the sentence is complete.","A word can be recognized before its role in the sentence is certain. The listener still has to determine which words belong together and how the event participants are related.","The central issue is how syntactic preferences, meaning, context, and expectations interact. Reading times, eye movements, actions, and brain responses reveal parts of that process.","Parsing supplies a structural analysis of that developing sequence. Ambiguity means that more than one structure may fit the words encountered so far.","The experiments ask when information from syntax, word meaning, scene context, and form expectations affects the choice among those possibilities. Their time-sensitive measures reveal more than a final grammaticality judgment."],"sentence-02":["The sentence can mean that the horse that was raced past the barn subsequently fell. Raced past the barn modifies which horse is being discussed; fell is the main predicate.","The horse raced past the barn fell contains a reduced relative: the horse that was raced past the barn fell. The omitted that was makes the modifying clause less explicit.","An initial interpretation instead treats raced as the main verb. On reaching fell, that analysis leaves no straightforward place for the new main event.","At the beginning, raced can be treated as the horse's main action. That locally plausible analysis creates a problem when fell arrives as the actual main predicate.","Adding that was after horse makes the modifying structure explicit. The difficulty therefore concerns resolving a **reduced relative clause**, not the impossibility of expressing the intended event.","Disambiguation restores the relationship between the clauses. One part identifies which horse is being discussed, while the other states what happened to that horse."],"sentence-03":["A **garden path** occurs when an initially attractive interpretation becomes incompatible with later input. The reader must revise the earlier structural assignment to continue.","The garden-path effect is the experience of being led into a locally attractive interpretation that later input makes difficult to maintain. It is a processing problem within a grammatical sentence.","In the horse example, the early main-verb reading of raced is the tempting path. The later fell forces raced to be reinterpreted as part of a modifying clause.","A reader does not normally hold all possible structures in equally detailed form. An early preference can be strong enough that a later word produces a need to retrace the analysis.","The experience shows that comprehension is incremental. The system commits enough to an interpretation during the sentence that later evidence can produce a measurable cost of reanalysis.","The famous horse sentence is informative because the point of difficulty is not a failure to recognize fell as a word. The difficulty concerns where that word belongs in the structure already being constructed."],"sentence-04":["A **parser** builds a structural analysis connecting the words of a sentence. The initial tree assigns raced to the main verb position and treats the following material accordingly.","The parser is implemented in a cognitive system with limited memory and time constraints. The proposed structural strategies make sense as ways of avoiding an unlimited search through alternatives.","Maintaining every possible tree would demand memory and time. An efficient system might prefer a simpler analysis and revise it only when later evidence makes that necessary.","The initial tree treats the sentence as a relatively straightforward main clause. It uses the developing structure already available rather than adding an embedded modifier immediately.","The account explains a garden path through a processing strategy. The sentence is grammatical under another structure, but the parser initially chooses a different one.","This turns economy into a prediction about early interpretation. The cost appears later if the selected structure cannot accommodate the incoming words."],"sentence-05":["**Minimal attachment** favors the analysis requiring fewer structural nodes. In the horse sentence, treating raced as the main verb initially builds a smaller tree than introducing a reduced relative clause.","The correct analysis of the horse sentence needs a modifying clause inside the larger noun phrase. That requires additional nodes connecting the horse with the raced-past-the-barn information.","When fell arrives, the smaller analysis fails. The correct structure has additional organization connecting the horse to a modifying clause before its main verb.","Initially preferring smaller trees is called minimal attachment. The preference concerns structural size, not simply whether the sentence describes a probable event.","The proposed cause of difficulty is therefore a preference for structural economy. Reanalysis requires undoing the earlier economical choice and constructing the larger alternative.","When the main verb fell appears, the smaller initial analysis has to be replaced. The processing difficulty is the consequence of this earlier economical commitment."],"sentence-06":["**Late closure** favors attaching incoming material to the structure currently being processed. A phrase tends to continue the current clause or phrase when a grammatical analysis permits it.","Late closure is a bias to keep the current clause open as long as possible. New material is associated with the structure currently being built rather than forcing an earlier boundary immediately.","In the burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite, the final phrase can attach to the action. Dynamite then specifies the instrument used to blow the safe open.","With the dynamite can join the large verb phrase beginning with blew open. On that analysis, dynamite is the instrument of the action.","An alternative attachment makes the phrase describe the safe. The experiment compares interpretations of a locally ambiguous phrase rather than sentences with different words in every position.","An alternative places the phrase within the noun phrase, identifying a safe with certain contents or properties. The trees make the different attachment locations explicit."],"sentence-07":["With the jewels can describe the safe's contents, but jewels are an unlikely instrument for blowing it open. The plausible meaning therefore favors noun attachment over the initially preferred action attachment.","The safe with the jewels favors an interpretation in which jewels describe the safe's contents. They are not a plausible means of blowing the safe open.","Reading-time and eye-movement measures show a cost when the sensible analysis conflicts with the preferred structure. The system briefly entertains a less sensible relation and then revises it.","If the processor initially attaches with the jewels to the action, the result is temporarily the wrong event relation. Later interpretation has to reattach it to obtain the sensible reading.","This is an argument about the order of information use. A syntactic preference can guide the initial analysis before plausibility redirects the interpretation.","Slower reading and altered eye movements provide evidence of that cost. They make a brief, unpreferred interpretation observable even when the final sentence is understood correctly."],"sentence-08":["An **autonomous parser** initially computes syntactic structure using its own structural preferences. Meaning is not the primary determinant of that first analysis.","The proposed parser deals exclusively with abstract syntactic structure during its initial computation. Minimal attachment and late closure describe its default operating preferences.","Minimal attachment and late closure provide efficient defaults. They avoid maintaining many complete alternatives by choosing an economical path until a problem appears.","Meaning can then expose a problem in that first analysis, producing revision. This is why the model can explain an initial odd interpretation followed by a sensible final one.","The account resembles the staged lumber-mill model: one kind of operation supplies an analysis for another to evaluate. Experiments then ask whether nonstructural information can affect the earliest choices themselves.","Ignore ambiguity until it becomes a problem summarizes the strategy's efficiency. The following comparisons ask whether semantic and contextual evidence sometimes changes the earliest preference instead."],"sentence-09":["Some reduced-relative sentences are easy to understand despite having the supposedly demanding structural form. Their words and likely meanings make the modifying interpretation natural.","The verbs in the example set introduce reduced relatives, just as raced can in the horse sentence. Yet many of the examples are readily understandable.","MacDonald, Perlmutter, and Seidenberg argue that semantic cues and learned distributional regularities influence parsing from early on. The same abstract construction can receive different support in different contexts.","Semantic factors and statistical regularities interact with parsing from very early on in the alternative proposal. A word's usual uses and the likely roles of its arguments can favor the modifying structure.","A structure's existence therefore does not uniquely predict its processing difficulty. The frequency and plausibility of its components can change how strongly the competing analyses are activated.","The hypothesis predicts that processing difficulty varies with lexical content even when abstract construction type is held similar. Structure and experience-derived support jointly shape the competition."],"sentence-10":["The table collects reduced-relative examples that readers encounter without an overwhelming garden path. Several come from discussions that otherwise use reduced relatives to support autonomous parsing.","The table includes ordinary examples drawn from discussions of reduced relatives. Their availability shows that readers encounter the construction in contexts where it is not overwhelmingly difficult.","Their shared structure is compatible with different experienced difficulty. This focuses attention on lexical and contextual differences instead of treating the construction as uniformly hard.","The comparison focuses on the distribution of difficulty across examples. If one structural description occurs in both easy and hard items, another source of support must help explain the difference.","The comparison motivates a **constraint-based** view: several partial sources of information favor or disfavor an analysis as the input unfolds.","This makes a constraint-based account concrete: semantic fit, lexical frequency, and context can make the intended structure strongly favored before the sentence ends."],"sentence-11":["In **“Recent research reported by…”**, several sources of information can support a reduced-relative interpretation very early. Research is inanimate and is unlikely to be the person doing the reporting, so its meaning favors research that someone else reported.","**Lexical statistics** add another cue: reported occurs frequently in reduced relatives. Experience with scientific papers makes that pattern especially familiar after research. The particular word, its usual construction, and knowledge about scientific writing can all support the same analysis.","These cues interact with syntax while the sentence is being processed. The reader does not have to wait for every word before information about likely events and familiar constructions becomes useful.","Compare **“The burglar arrested in the parking lot was charged today”** with **“The policeman arrested in the parking lot was charged today.”** In both, arrested introduces a reduced relative: the burglar or policeman is the person who was arrested.","A burglar is a more expected recipient of arrest, whereas a policeman is a plausible person doing the arresting. That difference can make the same grammatical continuation easier or harder to adopt. The example connects processing difficulty to the interaction of sentence structure, word statistics, and world knowledge."],"sentence-12":["Participants saw an apple on a towel, another towel, a box, and other objects. They heard put the apple on the towel in the box while their eye movements were measured.","The visual world contains an apple resting on a towel, an empty towel, a box, and another object. The participant hears an instruction and prepares to move the target.","On the towel can first sound like a destination for put. The correct instruction instead uses it to identify the apple, with in the box specifying the final destination.","On the towel can initially identify a destination for put or modify apple. The later in the box makes the final destination clear.","Looks to the empty towel reveal a temporary destination interpretation. The final action can be correct even though the eye trace records an earlier competing plan.","Looking at the empty towel reveals consideration of the destination interpretation before the action is completed. The competing plan becomes visible during processing, not just through a final error."],"sentence-13":["With only one apple available, the noun already identifies the target. A following location phrase is less needed to distinguish it from another apple.","With one apple, apple alone identifies the object. A following location description is not needed to distinguish it from another member of the same kind in the scene.","The empty towel can therefore attract looks as a possible destination while the instruction unfolds. Later in the box resolves the required movement.","That context leaves the destination interpretation of on the towel relatively attractive. The eye path can momentarily reflect a plan involving the empty towel.","The visual scene supplies information about what needs specifying. The ambiguity is not solely a property of the spoken words detached from their task context.","The display therefore supplies pragmatic information about why a modifier would be useful. The utterance is interpreted within an action context rather than as a detached string of words."],"sentence-14":["When two apples are present, on the towel becomes useful for identifying which apple to move. The phrase now has an immediate referential purpose.","Two apples create a referential distinction that must be resolved. On the towel now tells the listener which apple is intended instead of merely adding an unnecessary property to an already unique object.","Listeners make fewer looks to the irrelevant destination towel than in the one-apple situation. The spoken sentence remains the same while the visual context changes.","The prediction is less consideration of the empty towel as a destination. Eye movements show that reduction while the instruction is still unfolding.","This supports early use of **referential context**. The need to distinguish objects can change which structural interpretation is favored during processing.","The spoken ambiguity is unchanged, but the reason for using the phrase has changed. Early interpretation therefore responds to the immediate communicative scene."],"sentence-15":["The eye-movement comparison measures the temporary attraction of the wrong destination in one- and two-referent scenes. Its reduction with two apples shows context changing the evolving interpretation.","Different parses of the same sentence are favored under different semantic and pragmatic conditions. Pragmatic conditions concern what an expression accomplishes in the current situation.","A purely syntactic default would not straightforwardly predict a different early preference from the same string solely because another apple is present. The contextual distinction is informative before the sentence ends.","The one- versus two-referent comparison tests that claim without replacing the ambiguous words. The scene changes how useful a modifier is, which changes the competing interpretation.","Syntax and pragmatics therefore interact during comprehension. **Pragmatics** concerns how an expression serves the communicative situation, including why a speaker would specify one apple's location.","The early timing is central. Context does not merely repair a fully completed parse after the task; it changes the pattern of activity during the developing instruction."],"sentence-16":["Mouse tracking records the developing movement toward a response. A curved or diverted path can reveal competing interpretations that influence the action before it is completed.","Mouse movement measures the action continuously rather than recording only which location is eventually reached. A trajectory can show attraction toward an alternative destination.","The one-referent and two-referent contexts affect these trajectories, just as they affect eye movements. Ambiguity changes the execution of the action rather than only a retrospective report.","The one- and two-referent conditions change how the ambiguous phrase is interpreted while the movement is underway. The influence reaches actual arm movement, not only looking.","The method measures time course through movement. A correct endpoint does not necessarily mean there was never competition on the way to it.","Eye tracking and mouse tracking therefore provide converging windows on competition. The final destination can be correct even when the unfolding response reveals another temporarily active plan."],"sentence-17":["**Event-related potentials**, or ERPs, are brain electrical responses averaged relative to a defined event, such as a word appearing. Their timing helps identify when a manipulation affects processing.","A psychophysiological investigation relates a controlled language contrast to a time-sensitive physiological response. An ERP is averaged electrical activity aligned with the arrival of an event.","Syntax experiments compare a word in an expected versus an unexpected category position. The physical word and its sentence context together determine what counts as a violation.","Presenting a sentence word by word allows the response to an unexpected category to be examined at the moment the mismatch appears. Earlier context establishes what is expected.","An early response provides evidence of rapid expectation sensitivity. Explaining its mechanism requires examining both grammatical structure and properties of the word's form.","A fast effect is evidence about processing time. Its explanation depends on what category and physical-form information the experimental materials make available at that point."],"sentence-18":["The begins a noun phrase and creates expectations about what kinds of material can follow. No complete sentence meaning is available yet.","The signals a determiner and prepares a noun-phrase continuation. It begins an expectation rather than expressing a complete event by itself.","In word-by-word presentation, the response to each new item is interpreted relative to the material already seen. This lets the experiment measure the unfolding process instead of only the final judgment.","Incremental comprehension means using this partial information before the entire sentence arrives. The next word is processed against that developing context.","The developing prediction is provisional. Later words can specify, satisfy, or conflict with what the earlier context made likely.","In the word-by-word demonstration, the sequence makes the buildup visible. A later violation matters because preceding words have already established a structure in which it must fit."],"sentence-19":["Pizza supplies the noun following the determiner. The reader can now construct a noun phrase referring to a particular kind of entity.","Pizza fills the noun position opened by the. The developing phrase now identifies the entity about which the sentence will make a claim.","This is still not a complete proposition. The next words must establish what is said about the pizza and how the remaining sentence fits together.","The reader has recognized content, but the predicate and its structure are not yet complete. Several continuations remain possible at this stage.","Incremental presentation separates recognizing a word from integrating it into an evolving structure. The same word can be easy to recognize while its grammatical continuation remains open.","This separates access to a word's meaning from completion of the sentence. The experiment can then examine how each subsequent item changes what the context predicts."],"sentence-20":["Was changes the developing expectations for the predicate. It can participate in constructions describing a state or a passive event, depending on what follows.","Was is compatible with several kinds of continuation, including a state description or a passive construction. It contributes grammatical information even before the particular predicate is complete.","The reader uses the emerging phrase to predict compatible continuations. Those predictions do not have to wait until a whole sentence is visible.","The processor can therefore prepare for certain forms without knowing the sentence's final meaning. That preparation becomes more specific as further words arrive.","This stage prepares the later violation comparison. An unexpected item is defined by its relationship to the structure established so far.","The demonstration is about this changing expectation. The relevant contrast will arise when a later word conflicts with the structure established by the combined sequence."],"sentence-21":["In introduces a prepositional relation. A continuation can specify the location or context associated with the pizza.","In introduces a prepositional phrase, adding a relation that needs an appropriate complement. This changes which kinds of forms make sense immediately afterward.","The preposition changes which forms are expected next. It invites a phrase of an appropriate category rather than an arbitrary sequence of recognizable words.","A reader does not need to wait for every later word before using the preposition's grammatical information. The local sequence already constrains the continuation.","A category-sensitive response therefore depends on the prior context. Recognizing in also reorganizes predictions about the next part of the sentence.","That is why a category violation is contextual. The same recognizable word can fit one position and create a mismatch in another."],"sentence-22":["The pizza was in the now strongly sets up a noun-phrase continuation after the preposition. A noun would complete the immediate determiner–noun relation.","After in the, the immediate structure favors a noun phrase such as a location expression. The determiner supplies more specific category information than the preposition alone.","This is a specific expectation about grammatical category. It is different from merely guessing a semantically related word such as a common pizza topping.","The prediction concerns grammatical role rather than simply a meaning associated with pizza. A familiar food-related word would still have to occupy the required kind of position.","The ERP comparison asks what happens when the next item conflicts with that prepared category and whether its visible form contributes to the response.","This creates the background for the next word's effect. The experiment compares what is delivered with the physical and structural information the context has prepared."],"sentence-23":["Eaten does not supply the ordinary noun required by the immediately preceding in the sequence. The words are individually recognizable, but their combination disrupts the expected structure.","Eaten is a recognizable form, but it does not complete the ordinary noun continuation required after in the. The mismatch concerns its placement in the developing sequence.","The crucial event occurs when the violating item arrives. The reader can detect the mismatch before evaluating a long, completed passage.","The experimental event is the arrival of that category-inappropriate item. The preceding words explain why this occurrence is a violation rather than simply a word with an unusual meaning.","This separates lexical familiarity from structural fit. Knowing a word does not ensure that it belongs in the position where it is encountered.","A rapid response can reflect the failure of a prepared expectation. Subsequent studies ask whether the earliest effect depends on the word's visible category cues as well as its abstract role."],"sentence-24":["The **ELAN**, early left anterior negativity, is an ERP effect reported for word-category violations, with an early timing around 100–200 milliseconds. The name describes its timing, polarity, and scalp distribution.","The ELAN is elicited for word-class violations in the classic comparison. Early refers to its timing, left anterior to the reported scalp distribution, and negativity to the direction of the measured effect.","Sequences such as the pizza was in the eaten place an item where its grammatical category does not fit. Researchers compare responses with category-appropriate continuations.","The scientist criticized Max's of proof the theorem is another category-order violation. Its familiar individual words do not supply a well-formed sequence in those positions.","The timing motivated interest in rapid syntactic processing. Whether the effect identifies an autonomous grammar computation depends on what physical cues distinguish the compared words.","The theoretical interest is whether such an early response reflects syntax-specific processing. Testing form typicality asks what information permits the early system to detect the mismatch."],"sentence-25":["**Dikker and colleagues** connect early syntactic expectations with **form typicality**: how characteristic a word’s visible form is of a grammatical class. An expectation for a verb can prepare the visual system for patterns that verbs tend to have.","The prediction therefore reaches beyond an abstract noun-or-verb label. If grammatical expectations feed back into perception, an expected category can also prepare physical properties associated with that category.","An incoming word whose form conflicts with that expectation can produce an early visual response. This explains why sensitivity in visual cortex can arise before a complete analysis of the whole sentence.","The distinction between a strongly category-typical form and a neutral form matters. An obvious form cue provides a clearer opportunity for an expected visual pattern to be confirmed or violated.","The account links syntax to learned visual statistics. Grammatical context influences what form is expected, and the match between that prediction and the incoming word helps determine the early perceptual response."],"sentence-26":["The sentences use the same written words in different grammatical roles. **Perches** can name objects that birds sit on, or describe what a bird does. **Needs** can name requirements, or express needing something.","In **“Chris and Ben are glad that the bird perches seem easy to install,”** bird perches is a noun phrase. The perches are the things that seem easy to install, and the plural verb seem agrees with them.","In **“Chris and Ben are glad that the bird perches comfortably in the cage,”** bird is the subject and perches is its verb. Comfortably describes the action or position of the bird.","The same contrast appears in **“The teacher told the principal that the student needs were not being met”** and **“…that the student needs to be more focused.”** In the first, needs names the student’s requirements. In the second, needs is a verb describing what the student requires.","The continuation makes the category clear even though the critical written word is unchanged. This lets grammatical context be compared with expectations associated with the word’s form: the form can look more typical of one category while the sentence uses the other."],"sentence-27":["**Perches is noun-like, and needs is verb-like.** These labels describe the forms’ typicality, rather than a rule restricting each word to one grammatical use. Both words appear as nouns and as verbs in the sentence materials.","In **“the bird perches seem easy to install,”** the noun-like form perches is used as a noun. In **“the bird perches comfortably in the cage,”** that same form is used as a verb. The latter combines a noun-like physical pattern with a verbal role.","In **“the student needs were not being met,”** needs is a noun referring to requirements. Its verb-like form therefore appears in a nominal use.","In **“the student needs to be more focused,”** needs is used as a verb. Here its form typicality and grammatical role align.","Crossing the two properties separates the influence of sentence structure from the influence of word form. If early visual processing uses predicted physical patterns, the match between form typicality and the expected category should affect the response."],"sentence-28":["The four sentences separate **syntactic category** from **form typicality**. Syntactic category is the noun or verb role required by the sentence. Form typicality concerns whether the visible word looks more characteristic of nouns or verbs.","**“The bird perches seem easy to install”** uses perches as a noun, while **“the bird perches comfortably in the cage”** uses it as a verb. Because perches is noun-like, the second use pairs the same physical form with the less typical category.","**“The student needs were not being met”** uses needs as a noun, while **“the student needs to be more focused”** uses it as a verb. Needs is verb-like, so the nominal use reverses the alignment found for perches.","The comparison asks whether an early response follows abstract grammatical expectations alone or also the physical word patterns associated with those expectations. Reusing each critical form makes its grammatical role change without replacing the word itself.","A form-based account predicts that the visible cues matter to the early perceptual effect. Sentence processing can therefore influence perception through expectations about how the next word is likely to look, connecting grammatical knowledge with the analysis of written input."],"sentence-29":["The early effect appeared where a noun's category was supported by morphological marking or statistical form information. Neutral nouns without strong category cues did not show the same effect.","The ELAN effect is limited to cases with physically available category information in the reported comparison. That information can come from morphology or from statistical form-based nouniness.","**Morphological marking** supplies a meaningful form cue; statistical nouniness comes from the patterns shared by many noun forms. Both are physically available when the written word appears.","A neutral noun such as infant lacks the same strong cue. Its unexpected category does not produce an equivalent early effect in these data.","The result connects the early response to predicted physical form. It does not reduce all syntax to spelling; it explains why the earliest measured mismatch can depend on learned visual properties.","This connects the early response with learned perceptual predictions. The structural expectation matters, while the strength of its match to visible form determines what can be detected at that early moment."],"sentence-30":["Colorless green ideas sleep furiously separates grammatical organization from sensible meaning. The horse sentence shows that locally preferred structures can create garden paths.","Syntax is independent of semantics to some extent: grammatical organization can survive an anomalous meaning. The colorless-green example demonstrates that separation without making meaning irrelevant to processing.","Put the apple on the towel in the box shows early contextual influence. The bird perches example links grammatical expectations to the physical form of the upcoming word.","The horse and apple examples address how a structural analysis is reached. Preferred shortcuts can produce reanalysis costs, while local context can change which interpretation receives early support.","Together these cases describe incremental interpretation shaped by several constraints. A sentence's structure matters, while its words, scene, and expected forms help determine how that structure is reached.","The perches example adds prediction of physical word form. Together the four cases show a structured system whose online operation is influenced by several sources of information."],"sentence-31":["The aphasia material asks how the operations used in sentence comprehension change after brain injury. Structural ambiguity and role assignment provide useful test contrasts.","The aphasia reading applies these processing distinctions to acquired impairments. Sentence materials can vary whether roles are supplied by grammar or by likely event knowledge.","The connection is experimental: choosing sentences that separate syntax, plausibility, and processing demands makes it possible to explain specific patterns of comprehension difficulty.","That makes the transition from normal parsing to clinical evidence a continuation of the same experimental logic. Change a defined demand and examine the response pattern.","The next question is which parts of a person's difficulty concern particular linguistic operations and which can also arise from broader processing pressures."],"l2-learning-01":["An **L2** is a second language learned after another language is established. Learning draws on existing conceptual skills and linguistic representations while adapting to a new system.","Second-language acquisition begins with an already organized linguistic system. The learner brings familiar categories, forms, and meanings to the task of learning another language.","The central topics are transfer, competition between languages, and what happens when a previously used language stops receiving input.","That starting point can reduce uncertainty when systems overlap and create difficulty when they organize the same domain differently. Transfer is therefore central to both the advantages and the problems of later learning.","The experimental questions concern the mechanisms behind these effects. Similarity, competing activation, continued use, and changed exposure histories provide different routes by which L1 and L2 interact."],"l2-learning-02":["Adults often learn more quickly at the beginning of L2 instruction. They already possess conceptual knowledge, learning strategies, and an established language that can support the new task.","Adults initially learn more quickly than children, due largely to transfer from L1 and better general cognitive skills. The early rate is a measure of how rapidly progress occurs at the beginning.","**Ultimate attainment** is the level reached after extended learning. Earlier learners more often reach native-like outcomes even though they may initially progress more slowly.","Ultimate attainment is the eventual level after an extended learning history. Adults' early advantage does not guarantee native-like performance at that endpoint.","These measures answer different questions. A child advantage in eventual attainment does not imply that adults learn fewer words in every early lesson.","Transfer can contribute in both directions: existing knowledge can accelerate initial acquisition while making some new distinctions difficult to reorganize. The age comparison depends on which stage and outcome are measured."],"l2-learning-03":["A first-language learner is also developing concepts such as spatial relationships and events. An older L2 learner already has much of that knowledge available.","Spatial relations and event representations are general conceptual abilities as well as ingredients of language use. A young child is developing much of that conceptual organization alongside linguistic expression.","The later learner can focus on how the new language expresses familiar relations. This reduces some demands compared with learning both the concepts and their linguistic expression.","A later L2 learner can focus more on how another language expresses relations already understood. The new labels and structures do not have to create every underlying concept from nothing.","Existing language also shapes attention and expectations. The same prior experience that helps with one feature can interfere with a feature organized differently in L2.","The existing L1 adds a second influence. Its learned categories can support the new mapping or direct attention toward the wrong distinctions for the target language."],"l2-learning-04":["**Transfer** is using L1 processes and representations in L2. It is positive when the existing pattern fits the new language and negative when it creates a misleading expectation.","Transfer is the use of native-language processes and representations for a second language. The definition concerns reuse of an existing system, not only deliberate word-for-word translation.","Similar words or structures can reduce the amount that must be learned. Incompatible sound categories, agreement rules, or word meanings can instead create persistent errors.","A matching relationship gives positive transfer: something already learned fits the new task. A mismatching relationship gives negative transfer: the familiar pattern directs processing toward an inappropriate solution.","Transfer is therefore a relation between two systems. The question is which specific previously learned mapping is being reused and whether it matches the target language.","The effect is therefore specific to what is transferred. A learner can benefit from similarity in vocabulary while having difficulty with a sound contrast or grammatical dependency in the same L2."],"l2-learning-05":["Hindi distinguishes dental and retroflex consonants that English listeners may initially interpret using one alveolar category. **Place of articulation** describes where a consonant is produced.","English learners tend to use an alveolar category to interpret both dental and retroflex Hindi consonants. These categories distinguish where the constriction is made in the vocal tract.","Mapping both L2 sounds onto the same L1 category makes their difference less salient. The learner must establish a distinction that was not required in the same way by English.","Acquired similarity means that the two L2 sounds can be heard as variants of one familiar L1 category. The useful English grouping obscures a contrast required for Hindi word recognition.","This is negative phonetic transfer. Familiar categorization helps recognize speech generally but can merge two sounds that must be separated to distinguish L2 words.","Learning needs to make that distinction effective. The challenge is not simply hearing sound energy, but changing how two patterns are separated in the learner's existing perceptual organization."],"l2-learning-06":["Chinese classifiers depend on properties associated with the noun. Different nouns require different classifier choices in relevant constructions.","Different Chinese nouns require different classifiers in the relevant constructions. Choosing the form depends partly on noun properties that English does not use in the same way for a chair or that dog.","English determiner use does not demand that same system of semantic sorting. An English learner must start attending to distinctions that ordinary L1 grammar did not require in that position.","English speakers have learned to ignore those cues for that grammatical choice. The new language makes previously less useful information relevant to selecting a form.","The transfer problem is therefore not just remembering another short word. It involves learning which noun properties control a grammatical choice.","This is transfer in attention as well as in memory. The learner must discover which noun features control the classifier and then use that mapping reliably while speaking or understanding."],"l2-learning-07":["**Grammatical gender** assigns nouns to classes that affect related words. It is distinct from social gender and does not necessarily reflect a noun's real-world properties.","There is nothing inherently male about a book or female about liberty in the grammatical classification being discussed. The class is part of a language's organization rather than a simple description of the object.","Pronouns, adjectives, and some verb forms may have to agree with a noun's grammatical class. French synonyms can belong to different classes even when they refer to similar things.","Agreement means that related forms must coordinate with the noun's class. The consequences can involve pronouns, adjectives, and some verb forms, not only the noun itself.","A learner must acquire both class membership and the consequences for agreement. L1 habits can interfere when the first language does not organize those dependencies similarly.","French pairs such as auto/voiture and vélo/bicyclette illustrate why meaning alone does not determine the class. A learner needs the particular form's gender as well as the agreement relationships built from it."],"l2-learning-08":["Similar words across languages can facilitate vocabulary learning. A **cognate** reflects shared historical origin, while a **loan word** comes from borrowing.","Some cross-language similarity reflects shared etymology, meaning a common historical source. Other similarity results from more recent borrowing of vocabulary between languages.","The lecture examples include recognizable European forms and Japanese adaptations of borrowed vocabulary. Similarity can provide an initial connection between a new form and an already known meaning.","Auto, rivière, and bicyclette illustrate recognizable forms, while the Japanese examples show borrowed material adapted to another sound system. Familiar structure can supply an initial link to meaning.","This is positive transfer: prior representations reduce uncertainty. The useful claim concerns a helpful relation between form and meaning, not a guarantee that every familiar-looking form shares its meaning.","This is a beneficial use of the existing lexicon. The learner has less arbitrary form–meaning information to establish when the correspondence is useful."],"l2-learning-09":["A word that resembles an L1 form can have a different meaning. Spanish embarazado means pregnant, not embarrassed. Its familiar-looking form can therefore produce a confident but mistaken interpretation.","Not all similar-sounding words are cognates. A resemblance can activate a familiar meaning even when the new language uses the form differently.","The bias is understandable: many cross-language similarities are useful, so an existing word is quickly activated by a similar L2 form. The error occurs when the meaning relation does not match.","Spanish embarazado means pregnant rather than embarrassed. The English resemblance produces the mistaken interpretation in the Arnold example.","**False friends** therefore expose negative lexical transfer. Learning requires distinguishing useful correspondences from misleading ones rather than avoiding L1 knowledge altogether.","The same bias that helps with real correspondences can therefore generate confident errors. Successful learning must identify which apparent cross-language matches preserve the intended meaning and which do not."],"l2-learning-10":["**De Groot and Keijzer** studied transfer using **paired-associate learning of made-up words**. Dutch words were paired with unfamiliar associates, and participants were asked to learn which forms belonged together.","The pairs varied in similarity to their Dutch associates. **Gitaar–gettar**, meaning guitar, is a concrete cognate-like pair. **Tijger–duunze**, meaning tiger, is concrete but non-cognate: its new form provides much less resemblance to the familiar Dutch word.","They also varied **concreteness**. **Domein–domaan**, meaning domain, is an abstract cognate-like pair, whereas **herstel–groop**, meaning repair, is abstract and non-cognate. This crosses form similarity with the kind of concept being learned.","Participants see the paired forms and try to establish an association that can later be retrieved. Similarity can provide a bridge from a known word to the new label, while a dissimilar form requires learning a less familiar connection.","Testing after a week examines retention as well as immediate learning. Comparing cognate-like and non-cognate pairs within this design asks whether existing language knowledge helps acquire and remember new vocabulary from the earliest stages."],"l2-learning-11":["The reported pattern is that **cognates are learned more rapidly and remembered more easily**. Similarity between a familiar Dutch word and its newly learned associate helps performance during learning and later retention.","A cognate-like form provides a connection to knowledge already available. Recognizing that connection makes the unfamiliar label easier to link with an existing word and meaning.","**Session 4 follows one week without training.** Performance there therefore concerns how well the learned association persists, rather than the immediate benefit of seeing a pair again during practice.","The table also distinguishes concrete and abstract items. These comparisons retain the design’s separate factors, so the cognate effect concerns similar versus dissimilar forms within that broader set of materials.","The result places transfer at the beginning of learning. Existing form knowledge can help establish and later retrieve the new association before the learner has developed broad proficiency in the second-language vocabulary."],"l2-learning-12":["The paired-associate study distinguishes **receptive** from **expressive or productive** vocabulary testing. The two directions place different demands on what has been learned.","In the receptive direction described here, the new associate is supplied and the learner produces the Dutch word. A form resembling a familiar word can provide a strong clue about the intended answer.","Gitaar, tijger, and domein also resemble words that an English speaker might already recognize. Guessing a meaning from a familiar-looking form is therefore easier than establishing that its exact unfamiliar partner can be retrieved.","In productive testing, the familiar word is supplied and the learner has to produce the new associate. The participant must recall the learned form itself, rather than only recognizing which familiar meaning it resembles.","Including this direction strengthens the interpretation of a cognate learning benefit. Similarity helps not only recognition or guessing, but also the acquisition of a form that can be produced when the known word is given."],"l2-learning-13":["The table separates productive from receptive recall and immediate from delayed tests. Cognates outperform noncognates across these comparisons, with less forgetting over the delay.","The table crosses concrete versus abstract items with cognates versus noncognates and separates productive from receptive testing. These are distinct dimensions rather than alternative labels for one contrast.","Concrete items are also retained better than abstract ones in the displayed data. This is a second factor, so it should not be confused with cognate status.","Immediate recall shows what was available after learning, and delayed recall shows what survives the week. The drop across sessions provides an index of forgetting.","Productive success matters particularly because it requires retrieving a foreign-like form. The pattern supports both initial acquisition and retention advantages rather than an effect limited to guessing familiar meanings.","A cognate advantage in productive testing cannot be explained only by guessing a familiar meaning from a supplied similar form. The learner must recover the foreign-like item, supporting an effect on the acquired mapping."],"l2-learning-14":["The research question is whether an L2 word activates similar L1 words even when the task is conducted in L2. A visual-world display can make those potential competitors observable.","The question is whether similar L1 words are always activated when processing L2. An English task can be used to test activation of Russian candidates without asking for a Russian response.","A listener hears a target and looks among objects. Additional looks to an object whose L1 name overlaps with the input indicate cross-language competition.","The visual world supplies pictures whose names can differ in whether they compete within the task language or through the other language. An unrelated picture supplies a control level of looking.","The task therefore tests selective language access during ordinary word identification. Knowing which language is being used may not prevent all activation in the other lexicon.","The informative response is increased consideration of the cross-language competitor. A correct final L2 selection does not rule out another lexical system having participated earlier."],"l2-learning-15":["McMurray's bear–pear task showed that eye movements reveal partial competition hidden by the final click. The competitor becomes more attractive as acoustic input grows ambiguous.","Bear–pear competition varies with distance from a category boundary even when the participant finally chooses the same word. Eye movements make the changing strength of that alternative observable.","Bilingual experiments use the same logic with a different kind of competitor. A picture can compete because its name in another language resembles the spoken target.","The bilingual experiment extends the logic to a candidate supported through another language. The input–competitor relationship changes, but the time-sensitive response measure remains useful.","The measure concerns activation during processing. A correct L2 response can coexist with temporary attraction to an L1-linked object.","This makes cross-language activation a processing hypothesis rather than a demand to translate consciously. Looks can reveal a candidate that was briefly considered and then rejected."],"l2-learning-16":["A **cohort** consists of words compatible with the beginning of a spoken form. Early input activates more than one candidate, and later sounds reduce the competition.","An onset cohort is the set of words compatible with the beginning of a spoken target. Early evidence can support several members before later sounds separate them.","In a visual-world display, looks to a same-onset competitor rise and then decline as the target becomes clearer. The timing is important even if only one object is eventually selected.","The cohort competitor's looks rise while its onset fits and decline as incompatible input arrives. A final correct response compresses this history into one selected item.","This provides the baseline mechanism for the bilingual question. Cross-language cohorts extend onset competition beyond the language explicitly required by the task.","That temporal competition is the baseline for the bilingual study. A cross-language cohort can be activated because the initial sound matches a word in another lexicon."],"l2-learning-17":["Native Russian speakers processed English targets while viewing objects. A target such as speaker overlaps in sound with the Russian name for matches, making the matches picture a potential competitor.","A book of matches is a competitor for an English speaker target because its Russian name begins similarly. The relation belongs to the sound of the Russian label, not to the English labels being synonyms.","The picture's English name does not have to resemble the target. The phonological overlap arises through the other language.","The display therefore compares within-language and cross-language competitors against an unrelated object. More looks than the control suggest that the candidate has entered the recognition competition.","If looks increase relative to an unrelated control, recognition is not fully restricted to English candidates. The experiment can compare within-language and between-language competition in the same setting.","This tests whether knowing the task is in English fully restricts lexical activation. The relevant result can occur even when the participant understands the instruction and ultimately chooses the English target."],"l2-learning-18":["In the English task, listeners looked at both English and Russian-name competitors. With one competitor present, within- and between-language competition were comparable in the reported conditions.","With only one competitor present, within- and between-language objects attract comparable competition in the reported comparison. Both can be considered while the English target is recognized.","When both appeared together, the English competitor attracted more looks, but the Russian competitor still exceeded the control. Competition from the other language did not disappear.","When both are present, the within-language competitor receives more looks, while the between-language competitor still receives more than the unrelated control. Relative strength changes without eliminating cross-language influence.","The result supports nonselective activation during L2 recognition in this population. The task language shapes competition without perfectly isolating one lexicon.","The pattern therefore includes both task-language support and nonselective access. L2 recognition is not simply isolated from the established L1 lexicon."],"l2-learning-19":["When the native Russian speakers performed the task in Russian, the English competitor did not show a statistically reliable advantage over the control in the reported data.","The reverse task uses Russian, the participants' native language. English-name competitors are then the cross-language alternatives being tested.","This differs from the L2 task, where Russian-name competitors attracted looks. The comparison asks about the direction and strength of cross-language influence.","Their small numerical difference from the control is not statistically significant in the reported data. That should not be described as the same demonstrated effect as the English-task result.","An asymmetrical connection account can explain why L1 intrudes more into L2 than the reverse for these learners. The small plotted difference is not equivalent to evidence for a reliable competitor effect.","The asymmetry motivates a model with unequal link strengths. The learner's two languages can interact differently depending on which language is currently driving recognition."],"l2-learning-20":["The **Revised Hierarchical Model** links L1 words, L2 words, and shared concepts. The L1 lexicon is larger and more strongly connected to concepts in the learner configuration shown.","The L1 lexicon is assumed to be larger and more strongly connected to relevant concepts. Those are separate properties: the number of available words and the strength of their access to meaning.","L2 words have relatively strong links toward their L1 counterparts. Such a path is reinforced by learning a new word through a familiar translation.","L2 has strong forward connections to L1 because learning often emphasizes that direction, as when a new word is linked to a familiar translation. Activation therefore spreads more readily along one lexical route than the other.","Unequal link strengths make directional predictions. Activating L2 can engage L1 more readily than activating L1 engages L2 in this particular configuration.","These assumptions give the Revised Hierarchical Model a way to explain directional differences. The pattern of links reflects a history of learning and use rather than two identical isolated stores."],"l2-learning-21":["The English input can activate an overlapping Russian word. That lexical activation can in turn support the corresponding concept, making its pictured referent attract attention.","Once a competing Russian word for matches is partially active, it can support the concept of matches. That concept can then contribute to looking toward the pictured object.","Connections between words and concepts allow activation to circulate. Once the matches representation is partially involved, feedback can maintain its competition during the English task.","The proposed explanation includes feedback between lexical and conceptual activation. A partially supported candidate can gain further influence through its meaning even though it is not the required English response.","Weaker access from L1 into L2 helps explain the opposite-task asymmetry. The mechanism concerns routes and strengths of activation, rather than a claim that the two picture meanings must themselves be similar.","When the task is driven by the stronger L1, weaker links into L2 make the reverse intrusion less effective in this learner configuration. The model relates the observed asymmetry to how activation can travel through the network."],"l2-learning-22":["A **balanced bilingual** has relatively strong access to both languages. In an interactive account, stronger links do not necessarily eliminate competition.","Balanced bilinguals are not predicted to be two monolinguals in one. Strong access to shared concepts gives both languages opportunities to participate in processing.","If both languages connect effectively to shared concepts, activation and inhibitory control can be engaged across languages repeatedly. The person is not simply switching between two entirely isolated monolingual systems.","Competition and inhibition can therefore be engaged even when proficiency is high. Inhibition concerns controlling candidates that are active but inappropriate for the current task.","This prediction motivated work on control outside language. The theoretical prediction of ongoing competition should be distinguished from treating every possible nonlinguistic advantage as already established by these eye-movement data.","This motivated research on broader control abilities. The immediate theoretical prediction concerns interacting language systems; it is not a direct demonstration that every bilingual has every proposed nonlinguistic advantage."],"l2-learning-23":["The usual learning timeline adds L2 while L1 continues to be used. International adoption can instead sharply reduce or end contact with the original language.","In one timeline, L2 begins while L1 continues to be learned and used. Both systems keep receiving opportunities for activation.","This changes the input to an already organized system. Previously used connections may no longer be reinforced while the new language becomes dominant.","The alternative timeline ends continued L1 use after L2 is introduced. The changed environment no longer reinforces the earlier system in the same way.","The question is whether L1 is still accessible, becomes harder to access, or leaves traces that can help later relearning. These outcomes require different tests.","This creates a question about retention as well as acquisition. Later performance depends not only on the age of initial learning but on what input and practice followed it."],"l2-learning-24":["Adults adopted from Korea into French-speaking homes during childhood were tested for residual Korean knowledge. Their current language was French.","The adoptees entered French-speaking homes between early childhood ages and were tested as French-speaking adults. The study asks whether their earlier Korean experience leaves an accessible behavioral advantage.","Comparison with monolingual French speakers asks whether early Korean exposure confers a remaining advantage. Different tasks can probe sounds, words, sentence memory, and brain responses.","Comparing them with monolingual French speakers provides a baseline without that early history. Native Korean performance helps establish how the tested distinctions behave with continuing language experience.","The relevant history includes both early acquisition and later absence of input. This is a test of retention under a changed environment, not an ordinary classroom L2-learning trajectory.","Several tasks are needed because retained knowledge can concern phonetics, words, sentence memory, or the response to language in the brain. A history of exposure is not itself a measure of current accessibility."],"l2-learning-25":["Korean distinguishes three kinds of stop consonants in the contrast shown. A listener must differentiate the language's phonetic categories rather than merely detect that two recordings differ.","The Korean three-way stop contrast requires separating categories that French does not organize in the same way. The test therefore probes a specific potential remnant of early phonetic learning.","Early Korean experience might be expected to support this distinction later, even after long use of French. The comparison therefore probes a specific possible trace of the earlier sound system.","If early representations remain accessible, adoptees might distinguish those sounds better than French listeners without Korean childhood input. The prediction connects the exposure history with a measurable contrast.","Testing a well-defined contrast makes retention operational. Performance can be compared with listeners who have no comparable early Korean experience.","The goal is residual knowledge, not the ability to speak a complete Korean sentence. A narrow perceptual test can reveal an advantage even when general fluency has been lost, if that advantage is present."],"l2-learning-26":["The adoptees did not outperform monolingual French listeners on the tested Korean contrasts. Their early experience did not produce an accessible advantage in that task.","The tested Korean contrasts did not yield a reliable adoptee advantage over monolingual French listeners. Current performance therefore showed little evidence of preserved discrimination in that task.","The Korean comparison group supplies evidence that the contrast is measurable with relevant language experience. The key difference is therefore between retained current ability and a long-disused early history.","That is an observation about accessible behavior after a long period dominated by French. It differs from directly observing every earlier representation as it changed over the intervening years.","The finding concerns the tested phonetic discrimination. It motivates broader searches for residual knowledge rather than assuming that one negative task exhausts every possible form of retention.","The result motivates checking other functions and later relearning. Those measures can ask whether an early history has consequences that do not appear in immediate phonetic performance."],"l2-learning-27":["Pallier and colleagues tested language identification, Korean word translations, and detection of repeated excerpts in recorded sentences. These target different potential remnants of L1 experience.","The tasks included recognizing Korean among other languages, selecting Korean translations of French words, and detecting whether an excerpt had been repeated in a sentence. Each searches for a different possible trace.","The adoptees again did not show an advantage over monolingual French listeners. The tests sought any accessible trace rather than only performance on a demanding grammar test.","No advantage over monolingual francophones appeared in the reported comparisons. The search was deliberately broad rather than limited to advanced grammatical competence.","Agreement across several measures strengthens the observation of little detectable residual knowledge under those conditions. It also distinguishes current access from what later retraining might reveal.","Convergence across tests supports little accessible residual knowledge under those conditions. The subsequent material changes the learning conditions to ask whether earlier exposure can nevertheless influence reacquisition."],"l2-learning-28":["French activated familiar temporal and frontal language regions in the adoptees. Their current language therefore recruited a conventional language network.","French activated superior temporal and inferior frontal regions in the adoptees. The acquired dominant language therefore used the familiar broad network associated with language processing.","Contrasting French with Korean did not reveal the predicted residual Korean-processing difference between the groups. The imaging comparison converges with the behavioral tests' lack of a detected advantage.","A French-versus-Korean contrast was used to look for group differences that residual Korean processing might produce. The expected distinguishing advantage was not found in the reported comparison.","The result shows substantial reorganization with changed language experience. Brain responses and behavioral performance address related aspects of which language representations are currently effective.","Behavioral and imaging observations therefore point in a similar direction about current accessibility. The system's effective responses reflect the long-term shift in language experience."],"l2-learning-29":["Connections supporting a language gain strength through use. When L1 is no longer activated and L2 dominates experience, the competitive balance can shift toward the new system.","L1 can be represented as connections within and between regions rather than as a fixed object stored independently of use. Repeated activation helps those connections remain effective.","The lecture compares this with experience-dependent competition in visual development. A previously advantaged representation can lose effective access when another pattern is consistently reinforced.","If the earlier language is never activated again, the competing L2 organization can become dominant. The analogy is to experience-dependent competition in the visual system.","This provides a process for apparent replacement. It does not require a whole language to disappear instantaneously when adoption occurs; changing use alters the system over time.","Replacement is therefore described as a changing balance of effective pathways. The later question is whether a disadvantaged earlier pattern can still influence learning when the right input is restored."],"l2-learning-30":["Failure to detect a skill in an initial task does not settle whether early experience influences future learning. A later learning phase can test how readily the relevant patterns return.","Salvaging a trace requires a test that can reveal more than spontaneous current performance. A later training phase asks how easily the earlier pattern can be learned again.","**Unmasking** means making an earlier influence observable under conditions that were not present in the first test. The next example examines people with childhood exposure who later studied the language.","The question changes from can the person do it now to does early exposure affect what happens with new instruction. Those are different opportunities for prior experience to become observable.","The question changes from what can be performed now to what prior experience contributes to subsequent acquisition. Those are related but distinct measures of retention.","A residual representation can be masked in an initial test and still contribute during relearning. That possibility motivates the childhood-overhearer comparison."],"l2-learning-31":["Students who often overheard Spanish as children but did not learn to speak it later took classroom Spanish. Their childhood exposure preceded comparable later instruction.","The students overheard Spanish frequently as children but did not learn to speak it. They later began classroom instruction and were tested after college learning.","Performance after later learning can reveal benefits not obvious in initial fluent production. The early experience may preserve useful phonological information even without a previously spoken vocabulary.","Early hearing and early speaking are different histories. The comparison asks whether prior auditory exposure can provide an advantage when active language learning begins later.","The comparison addresses residual learning through later training. Childhood hearing and childhood speaking are different histories, so the type of early experience must be specified.","Later training may make an early influence visible that ordinary initial production does not reveal. This connects retention with the efficiency and outcome of reacquisition."],"l2-learning-32":["The Korean adoption tasks showed little accessible early-language advantage, while the overhearer work indicates that early exposure can affect later learning. These outcomes probe different forms of retention.","Complete removal of L1 from the environment can make L2 appear to replace it in current performance. The adoptee tests illustrate that observed outcome under several measures.","A representation may be difficult to use spontaneously but still influence relearning. Current proficiency and learning efficiency should therefore be measured separately.","Replacement does not necessarily mean that every influence of early experience is forever inaccessible. The overhearer comparison shows why later learning can provide another window on residual knowledge.","The synthesis connects competition with persistence. New-language dominance can coexist with early traces whose contribution becomes visible only under another learning condition.","The synthesis therefore separates present ability from future learning consequences. A theory of retention must explain both what is expressed immediately and what becomes available with renewed experience."],"l2-learning-33":["Korean exposure in France differs from Spanish exposure in Los Angeles. Continued opportunities to hear a language, family use, cognates, and a shared writing system can change later outcomes.","Korean is rarely encountered in the adoptees' French environment, while Spanish can remain available around a learner in Los Angeles. Continued incidental input can help maintain an earlier pattern.","These are mechanisms affecting maintenance and transfer, not interchangeable background details. A heritage-language environment can reinforce connections that complete absence of input does not.","A heritage language used by family members also supplies a different social setting from a language spoken by no one in the learner's current life. Cognates and a shared writing system add further routes for transfer.","Comparing learner groups requires tracking those histories. The effect attributed to early experience also depends on what happened between that experience and the eventual test.","These differences affect the learning mechanisms themselves. Comparing groups requires following their ongoing exposure and cross-language support, not treating early age as the only variable in the history."],"l2-sensitive-01":["L2 sensitive-period research asks how the age of learning relates to eventual proficiency. Learners already possess an L1, making this a different comparison from delayed first-language access.","Sensitive-period research asks how the effectiveness of learning changes with developmental state. An L2 learner differs from a delayed-L1 learner because an earlier language has already been established.","Age of arrival, task demands, transfer, and experience all contribute to interpreting the data. The central skill is explaining the pattern rather than assuming that one correlation names its cause.","Ultimate attainment concerns what is achieved after a learning history, while age of arrival indexes when substantial exposure began. Neither measure is identical to initial learning speed.","The explanation must connect a proposed mechanism with the shape of the age relationship and with the particular task used to measure it."],"l2-sensitive-02":["Lenneberg connected language-learning capacity to the development of cerebral dominance. The proposal combined milestones with evidence of greater early resilience after brain injury.","Cerebral dominance refers to the development of a lateralized organization for language. Lenneberg proposed that changes in this organization were relevant to the end of a favorable acquisition window.","A laterality account makes maturation part of the learning explanation. As organization changes, the opportunities for reorganization may also change.","The argument combined a rough alignment of brain development and language milestones with observations of recovery after brain injury or surgery. It was a synthesis across kinds of evidence.","L2 data test the broader timing prediction in learners with different ages of onset. They do not directly measure cerebral dominance simply by measuring grammar accuracy.","L2 attainment offers another way to examine an age prediction. A relationship between age and performance is relevant to the proposal, while the underlying neurological mechanism remains a distinct part of the explanation."],"l2-sensitive-03":["Severely delayed L1 cases are unusual and often involve complicated histories. L2 acquisition occurs at many ages in much larger populations.","L1 sensitive-period evidence often comes from idiosyncratic cases or rare phenomena. Those cases make unusual timing observable but bring complex histories with them.","Comparing later attainment across these learners can reveal age-related patterns. It also adds variation in prior language, instruction, and amount of experience.","Many people learn a second language at different ages, creating a wider range of onset experiences to compare. Native-like attainment is less common in later learners, making the limits especially interesting.","The research question becomes whether a timing effect remains informative about learning capacity when those histories and the measurement task are considered.","The larger comparison does not erase differences in language pairing, instruction, or exposure. It allows those factors to be investigated alongside timing rather than assuming that every late learner had the same history."],"l2-sensitive-04":["Johnson and Newport compared English attainment among Chinese- and Korean-speaking learners who arrived at different ages. A grammaticality-judgment task assessed several English structures.","Critical Period Effects in Second Language Learning frames the Johnson and Newport question as a comparison of attained English proficiency across ages of arrival. The learners already spoke Chinese or Korean.","**Age of arrival**, or AOA, provides an index of when substantial exposure began. **Attainment** refers to the level eventually measured, rather than how quickly the first lesson was learned.","The proposed developmental prediction is that earlier onset supports a more native-like eventual grammar. A grammaticality-judgment task makes that prediction measurable through responses to several constructions.","The proposed prediction was higher final proficiency after earlier arrival. The age pattern, including its shape before and after adolescence, is central to evaluating that proposal.","AOA is therefore related to a later behavioral outcome, not observed as a neural switch. The explanation depends on how the outcome varies through the early and late arrival ranges."],"l2-sensitive-05":["Participants listened to recorded sentences and reported whether each was grammatical. The test was **untimed**, allowing reflection instead of requiring an immediate interpretation in conversation.","Participants listened to recorded sentences and decided whether each sentence was grammatical. Grammaticality concerns the relevant structure rather than whether the sentence describes a sensible real-world event.","The example sets manipulate grammatical properties such as determiners, agreement, or questions. A correct response requires recognizing the particular contrast being tested.","The sample sets test specific contrasts, such as the use of determiners or the form of a question. Comparing the acceptable and unacceptable members helps identify which grammatical relation is being assessed.","This is a judgment about language, called **metalinguistic** performance. It can draw on explicit rules as well as the knowledge used during ordinary comprehension.","The test was untimed. That feature gives participants an opportunity to reflect consciously on a rule, which becomes important in interpreting what kind of knowledge the score measures."],"l2-sensitive-06":["Each point represents a learner's accuracy in relation to age of arrival. A downward relationship means that later arrival tends to accompany lower final judgment accuracy.","The vertical dimension records accuracy, and the horizontal dimension records age of arrival. A point locates one participant's attainment in relation to that arrival age.","The early and late ranges must also be examined separately. An overall correlation can conceal a change from an age-related slope to broad variability without a clear slope.","Looking only at the overall downward relation can miss a difference between subranges. A systematic early trend and a later scattered pattern are not the same relationship.","Describe where near-native performance occurs and how the distribution changes. The plot's shape carries more information than a statement that younger is better.","Describe where native-like scores are concentrated, where accuracy declines with age, and where later outcomes vary without a clear slope. Those features are what the competing explanations need to account for."],"l2-sensitive-07":["Early arrivals often perform close to the native range, particularly those arriving in early childhood. Accuracy declines across later childhood arrival ages in the presented data.","Early learners up to about eight are often near-perfect in the presented data. Arrival later in childhood is associated with declining accuracy before the broadly scattered late group.","Late arrivals show a wide spread of scores with little clear within-group relationship to age. Some perform much better than other late learners.","Nearly random distribution of accuracy for late arrivals means that the late scores are not tightly ordered by AOA. It does not mean that every participant was responding randomly to each sentence.","This is not a claim that late learners respond randomly or cannot learn grammar. The randomness concerns the scatter of attainment with respect to arrival age within that later range.","The contrast combines an early developmental relationship with later individual variation. Both parts matter to the sensitive-period interpretation developed from the graph."],"l2-sensitive-08":["Johnson and Newport interpret the age-related early decline as a developmental reduction in ordinary language-learning capacity. The later scatter is attributed to greater dependence on individual learning abilities.","Dependence of ultimate attainment on AOA is interpreted as a loss of language-learning ability over development. The proposal concerns a change in the usual acquisition process across the earlier range.","The explanation predicts both a timing relationship and a change in what determines success later. It is more specific than simply noting a mean difference between children and adults.","After the proposed window, attainment is argued to depend more strongly on idiosyncratic learning abilities. This accounts for the wide later variation rather than predicting one identical late score.","Some late acquisition remains possible. The pattern concerns typical ultimate attainment and its determinants rather than a strict statement that every postpubertal learner has zero sensitivity to language.","The theory therefore explains a pattern, not just a young–old average difference. It makes a claim about how the determinants of learning change over development."],"l2-sensitive-09":["Two versions of the **critical-period hypothesis** differ in what early language experience is supposed to preserve. Both begin with the proposal that humans have a superior capacity for acquiring languages early in life. The disagreement concerns what happens to that capacity as the person develops.","In **the exercise hypothesis**, the capacity declines if it is not exercised during the early period. Exercise here means using the capacity to acquire a language. The proposal is therefore about an interaction between early experience and later learning potential, rather than age acting independently of use.","If the capacity is exercised early, **further language-learning abilities will remain intact throughout life** under that hypothesis. A person who acquired a first language normally would therefore have preserved the ability needed for later language acquisition. Someone deprived of early accessible language would have a different prediction.","In **the maturational-state hypothesis**, the superior capacity disappears or declines with maturation. Early use does not prevent that developmental change. A person can acquire an L1 successfully in childhood and still approach a later L2 with a less favorable learning state.","Johnson and Newport’s participants already had a first language before learning English. The poorer ultimate attainment of many later arrivals therefore cannot be attributed simply to never having exercised language acquisition. This makes their pattern more directly consistent with the maturational-state version than with complete lifelong preservation through early exercise.","The comparison also changes how delayed-L1 evidence is interpreted. Difficulty after early deprivation can fit a lack-of-exercise explanation or a maturational one. Late-L2 learning after a successful early L1 separates those predictions more clearly because early linguistic exercise is already present."],"l2-sensitive-10":["An untimed grammaticality judgment allows conscious reflection on learned rules. A person can deliberately evaluate a sentence rather than process it at ordinary conversational speed.","Metalinguistic knowledge is knowledge used to think or talk explicitly about language. A participant can consult a consciously learned rule while making an untimed judgment.","**Explicit knowledge** is knowledge available to conscious explanation or monitoring. **Implicit knowledge** can guide fluent performance without a verbalized rule.","Fluent comprehension and production often depend on using the system without pausing to state the rule. A task that permits deliberation can draw on a different mixture of these abilities.","A judgment task may combine these sources differently from spontaneous use. The measured age effect therefore belongs to a particular task and cannot automatically be assigned entirely to implicit grammar acquisition.","The test-design issue therefore concerns what the measured accuracy represents. A judgment score can reflect classroom knowledge as well as the organization used in spontaneous language use."],"l2-sensitive-11":["The lecture relates untimed GJT scores to years of formal L2 education. Schooling can strengthen knowledge of rules that are directly useful for deliberative judgments.","Years of formal education in L2 correlate with untimed GJT performance in the reported comparison. Schooling is a plausible source of explicit knowledge about forms and rules.","This fits the proposal that an untimed test draws substantially on explicit metalinguistic learning. A well-practiced rule explanation may help even when conversational performance differs.","This supports the suggestion that the task may measure metalinguistic learning especially well. A participant can use that knowledge even when fast spontaneous performance presents different demands.","The association makes task demands part of the attainment interpretation. Comparing timed, spontaneous, and untimed measures helps clarify which kind of knowledge is producing a score.","The result connects an individual history with a task property. Interpreting an age relationship requires considering which kinds of learning the response format makes useful."],"l2-sensitive-12":["Languages differ in how many features map conveniently onto English. Similar vocabulary or constructions can support learning, while absent or incompatible features add difficulty.","Typological similarity concerns similarities in how languages organize their structures. A more similar L1 can provide forms or relationships that transfer usefully into English.","Johnson and Newport's learners had Chinese or Korean as L1. Another language pairing can change the available transfer even at comparable ages of arrival.","The original participants had Chinese or Korean as L1. Their learning problems are not necessarily identical to those of a speaker whose L1 already supplies a closer counterpart to an English construction.","An age effect must therefore be interpreted with the language pair in view. Maturation and prior representations both affect what an L2 learner must reorganize.","Transfer is therefore part of the attainment explanation. A similar age can coexist with a different amount of new structural organization required by the particular language pair."],"l2-sensitive-13":["Progressive -ing and word order are relatively successful structures in the displayed results. Other properties, such as determiners, are harder for learners whose L1 lacks the relevant system.","Progressive -ing and word order are among the easier structures in the displayed results. Other features vary more across onset ages and language backgrounds.","Questions can be relatively successful for late learners despite emerging late in children's L1 development. L2 learning does not simply replay the entire L1 developmental order.","Chinese has no corresponding determiner system in the contrast emphasized, creating a particular learning demand in English. Questions can be comparatively successful for late learners even though they emerge late in children's L1 development.","The pattern supports examining individual constructions. A total score combines different demands and different opportunities for transfer.","These different patterns show why a total grammar score should be unpacked. L2 acquisition is influenced by prior knowledge rather than automatically repeating the exact developmental order of L1 acquisition."],"l2-sensitive-14":["The comparison with Spanish-speaking learners changes the relation between L1 and English. Their attainment distribution differs from the Chinese/Korean pattern used in the original argument.","Birdsong and Molis compare Spanish-speaking learners of English with the earlier Chinese/Korean pattern. The change in population also changes potential cross-language correspondences.","Similarity between languages can change learning difficulty and the observed AOA relationship. The plots therefore test whether one age pattern generalizes across learner populations.","If the age–attainment relation differs, that difference bears on a claim that the observed curve is produced by maturation alone. Transfer and the tested constructions can alter what is difficult.","A maturational account must explain this dependence on language pairing as well as any overall earlier-learning advantage. Transfer contributes directly to the outcome being measured.","The point is to explain how an age effect generalizes across language pairings. A maturational account must be related to those differences rather than treating every group as an identical learning problem."],"l2-sensitive-15":["Later onset is generally associated with lower L2 attainment, but the pattern depends on constructions, test demands, and language pairings. These factors change what a score represents.","What constructions are tested, how they are tested, and the language pairings all affect observed attainment. Each changes the knowledge or processing demands needed for a successful response.","Age also covaries with experience: instruction, opportunities for use, and immersion differ across histories. Separating those influences requires more than reading a single downward trend.","Age of acquisition is also hard to dissociate from the amount and quality of experience. Earlier and later arrivals can have different educational, social, and usage histories.","A useful explanation identifies the proposed mechanism and its prediction. Maturation, transfer, and explicit learning can each contribute, so their distinct effects must be matched to the evidence.","The analysis therefore asks what each proposed factor predicts. Maturation, transfer, and task-specific explicit learning can make different contributions to the measured pattern."],"l2-sensitive-16":["The source assigns the reading chapter next. Reading adds another input modality in which form, learned representations, and experience interact during recognition.","The next reading topic moves to a different input modality while retaining questions about experience and representation. Recognizing written forms also depends on learned relationships among perceptual and linguistic levels.","The present midterm workspace stays with the uploaded speech, development, sentence, aphasia, and L2 material. Later reading lectures can be added as a new course module when their sources are supplied.","The current L2 discussion has shown why task definition matters: a judgment, a word-recognition measure, and spontaneous speech can expose different aspects of the same learner's system.","That experimental principle carries forward. A new domain should be studied by identifying the input, the requested behavior, and the proposed mechanism relating them."]},"references":{"notes11":{"file":"sources/notes11.pdf","original":"LING406_Lecture11_Notes (1).pdf","pages":12,"sha256":"1410f806ad9ef01a1d319fe6aaa07cfee41d2a1019de08eb06be447d8c679775"},"notes12":{"file":"sources/notes12.pdf","original":"LING406_Lecture12_Notes (1).pdf","pages":9,"sha256":"c82e84e68867090bf2eb5515740f8e5896bd87a698d0dbc1533dd253c9dd13b1"},"notes13":{"file":"sources/notes13.pdf","original":"LING406_Lec13_Aphasias_Notes (1).pdf","pages":14,"sha256":"412fb6e2e864102742b9abc1b3426c7dc42fa658fa78c71a3f1648ece5630453"},"notes14":{"file":"sources/notes14.pdf","original":"LING406_Lec14_LangDevelopment_Notes (1).pdf","pages":12,"sha256":"0ec96aa60be13d1bffa98e835c127cf37b4cbf1bf113f6539b623e810b920963"},"lecture14":{"file":"sources/lecture14.pdf","original":"LING406_Lec14 (1).pdf","pages":12,"sha256":"df933e3357e430fd4117de07f9c610140476d5af42d098546e576a2c7e69bee7"}}};
